Great — I can help you shape both the quantitative scenarios and the finance narrative for your internal materials. Below I give (A) a short summary of the current position, (B) clear, quantified runway-extension scenarios (cost-side, revenue-side, combined), (C) specific cost-reduction and revenue-acceleration levers with estimated impact and tradeoffs, (D) funding options, and (E) a recommended prioritized action plan and a short, ready-to-use finance narrative you can drop into a slide or memo. If you want, I can also draft the one-page slide or an email to execs from this.

A — Current snapshot (base assumptions)
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Stated net burn: $62,000 / month
- Runway (C / burn): 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (you reported ~20 months — consistent)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → ≈ $223,333 / month
- Implied total cash outflows (operating spend) ≈ revenue + burn ≈ 223,333 + 62,000 ≈ $285,333 / month

B — Runway sensitivity (simple, easy-to-present numbers)
1) Reducing net burn (B = $62k)
- No change: 19.4 months
- 10% burn reduction → new burn = $55.8k → runway ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 20% → $49.6k → runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% → $43.4k → runway ≈ 27.6 months (+8.2 months)
- 50% → $31.0k → runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.4 months)

2) Headcount / payroll example (illustrative)
- Assume payroll is 50% of total outflows (payroll ≈ $142.7k / month). If you reduce payroll:
  - 10% cut → saves ≈ $14.3k / month → new burn ≈ $47.7k → runway ≈ 25.1 months
  - 20% cut → saves ≈ $28.5k / month → new burn ≈ $33.5k → runway ≈ 35.9 months
  - 30% cut → saves ≈ $42.8k / month → new burn ≈ $19.2k → runway ≈ 62.5 months
(Note: adjust payroll share if your actual payroll is different; I can re-run with your payroll number.)

3) Revenue growth (no change in outflows)
- 10% lift in monthly revenue (+$22.3k) → burn declines to ≈ $39.7k → runway ≈ 30.3 months (+10.9 months)
- 20% lift (+$44.7k) → burn ≈ $17.3k → runway ≈ 69.3 months
- Break-even (net burn = 0) requires revenue increase ≈ burn / current monthly revenue = 62 / 223.33 ≈ 27.8% (i.e., ~28% more monthly revenue)

4) Combined example (costs + revenue)
- Example: 15% cost reduction (across non-revenue expenses) + 10% revenue growth:
  - Cost reduction saves ≈ 0.15 * (outflows) ≈ 0.15 * 285.3 ≈ $42.8k/month (not all of which may be cash savings depending on timing); revenue +$22.3k → combined burn reduction ≈ $65.1k (this would turn net burn negative — i.e., positive cash flow). (Note: this is illustrative; be careful not to double-count categories.)

C — Concrete levers to extend runway (prioritized, with estimated impact and tradeoffs)
Immediate / low-friction (0–30 days)
- Hiring freeze (save immediate future payroll commitments) — impact: low-to-medium; signal: strong. Quick to implement.
- Pause non-critical contractor spend and hiring for open roles — quick savings, but risk on delivery.
- 30–60 day vendor payment-term extensions and renegotiation of SaaS contracts — immediate cash timing relief; saves cash in the short-term but is temporary.
- Accelerate collections: shorten invoice terms, offer small discounts for faster payment — converts receivables to cash quickly; immediate impact if AR exists.
Estimated impact: combined could save $20k–$100k/month depending on scale and AR.

High-impact (30–90 days)
- Vendor renegotiation and vendor consolidation (target 10–30% on big contracts) — medium-term and can be durable.
- Reduce discretionary spend (travel, events, training, tools) — predictable small-to-medium savings.
- Reduce marketing spend channels with poor ROI; reallocate to high-roi channels — risk to top-line if overdone.
Estimated impact: $30k–$150k/month depending on contract mix.

Structural / people decisions (careful, 60–90 days)
- Targeted headcount reductions (goal: preserve core revenue-generating and product teams). If payroll is ~50% of spend, 15–20% cuts can materially reduce burn and buy many months.
Tradeoffs: morale, lost skills, slower roadmap.
- Temporary salary reductions or deferred bonus payouts for execs/leadership (used with clear sunset) — preserves workforce, immediate savings.
Estimated impact: large; each 10% company-wide payroll reduction yields big burn reduction as shown earlier.

Revenue acceleration (30–120 days)
- Push enterprise deals, accelerate renewals, upsell/cross-sell to existing customers (much higher conversion and faster cash than new logo acquisition).
- Introduce short-term pricing incentives for committed longer-term contracts.
- Evaluate quick-launch revenue streams (professional services, implementation fees).
Impact: A sustained 10–20% revenue lift dramatically extends runway (see B).

One-offs & financing
- One-time cost cuts (capex deferment, sale of non-core assets) — immediate but finite.
- Revenue-based financing or factoring (if you have recurring revenue/AR) — quick cash but expensive.
- Venture bridge / convertible note or venture debt — preserves equity (relative), adds runway; expect covenants and interest/fees.

D — Financing math and suggested raise targets
If you want to target a specific runway after raising:
- Target 36 months total runway at current burn: required cash = 36 * 62k = $2,232,000 → raise ≈ $1,032,000
- Target 48 months: required cash = 48 * 62k = $2,976,000 → raise ≈ $1,776,000
Notes:
- Raise sizes should include a 20–30% cushion for model error and hiring / growth plans.
- Because your current runway is already long (~20 months), you can be selective: consider a smaller bridge + milestones rather than a full growth round now.

E — Recommended prioritized action plan (what to do now)
Next 7–14 days (Immediate)
1. Announce a temporary hiring freeze and require CFO sign-off for new spend > $X.
2. Launch a 30-day vendor/SaaS review: identify top 10 vendors and aim for 10–25% savings or payment deferrals.
3. Run AR/collections push (sales + finance): target to recover any slow-paying accounts.
4. Prepare a 30/60/90-day cost-savings target (e.g., identify $50k/month in low-friction savings in 30 days).
5. Set weekly cash cadence: CFO presents cash balance vs. burn each week.

Next 30–90 days (Short-term)
1. Implement prioritized cost reductions (contractors, non-core marketing, discretionary spend).
2. Develop revenue acceleration programs (enterprise-focused, upsell offers, short-term pricing for extended contracts).
3. Model multiple scenarios (best, base, downside) and pick a fundraising trigger (e.g., raise if cash < 12 months or if growth/milestones missed).

60–120 days (Medium)
1. If required, prepare a bridge term sheet or approach lenders/investors — you already have time; use it to get better terms.
2. Operationalize longer-term structural savings (procurement, headcount rebalancing if needed).

F — Finance narrative (short paragraph + bullets you can paste into a slide/memo)
Suggested short narrative for leadership presentation:
"Stellar Labs enters this planning period with $1.2M cash and an estimated 20 months of runway at our current net burn of $62K/month. To preserve runway while protecting core growth initiatives, Finance proposes a two-track plan: (1) immediate, low-friction savings (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiations, collections acceleration) to secure 30–90 day cash upside, and (2) targeted medium-term actions (contractor rationalization and prioritized reductions in discretionary spend) to sustainably lower our monthly burn by 15–30% while we pursue revenue acceleration through upsell and enterprise deals. Under conservative combined actions (15% cost reduction + 10% revenue lift), we can extend runway substantially (moving toward positive cash flow). We will maintain weekly cash reviews and present updated scenario analyses monthly. If progress against milestones lags, we will initiate a targeted financing plan with a bridge to ensure 12–18 months of runway beyond the next milestone."

Suggested bullet points for execs
- Current: $1.2M cash / ~20 months at $62k/mo net burn.
- Immediate asks: hiring freeze + CFO approval for >$X; 30-day vendor review; AR acceleration push.
- Targets: identify $50–100k/month of durable savings in next 60 days; increase revenue by 10–20% through focused upsell/renewal programs.
- Governance: weekly cash review, monthly scenario update, fundraising trigger at X months / if Y milestones not met.

G — Next steps I can do for you (pick any)
- Draft a one-page slide or memo with the numbers and narrative above
- Produce a 3-scenario slide (base / best / downside) with a small chart showing runway under each scenario
- Re-run the analysis with your actual payroll number, top vendor spend, or AR balance to make the headcount and vendor-savings estimates precise
- Draft an internal email announcing the hiring freeze + immediate actions

Which of the next steps would you like me to do? If you want slides or a memo, tell me: (a) whether you want conservative or aggressive assumptions, and (b) actual payroll monthly amount or % (if available) so I can make the headcount scenarios precise.

