Nice — good data. I’ll give (A) a short, CEO-ready finance narrative you can drop into internal materials, (B) a set of specific runway-extension levers (revenue + financing + cost) with pros/cons and estimated impact, (C) quick scenario math so you can see how much runway each action buys, and (D) recommended next steps, owners and KPIs.

A. Short finance narrative (intro / talking points)
- Current position: “Stellar Labs enters the quarter with $1.2M in cash and an operating cash burn of ~$62K/month, giving us ~19–20 months of runway at current run rate. Revenue last quarter was $670K (≈$223K/month).”
- Objective: “Our near-term goal is to extend runway to a minimum of 24 months while continuing to invest in the highest-return revenue initiatives. We will execute a combined strategy of revenue acceleration, targeted cost optimization, and opportunistic financing to achieve conservative runway targets (24–36 months).”
- High-level plan: “1) Prioritize revenue acceleration (collections, pricing, customer expansion); 2) Implement controlled cost reductions focused on non-core discretionary spend and contractors; 3) Secure a backstop financing option (convertible/venture debt/bridge) if helpful; 4) Provide monthly transparency on cash, committed vs discretionary spend, and revised forecasts.”
- Ask for leadership: “Approval for a 60-day action plan (hiring freeze, renegotiate top vendor contracts, launch revenue sprints), and authority for Finance + HR to execute immediate operational savings.”

B. Levers to extend runway (with typical timing & expected impact)
1) Revenue acceleration (high ROI, moderate speed)
   - Short-term collections push: reduce Days Sales Outstanding (DSO) via early-pay discounts or collections outreach. Impact: improves cash balance fast; can free $10K–$50K+/month depending on receivables. Timing: 0–30 days.
   - Upsell/renewal campaigns for top customers: prioritized 10–20% lift in short-term revenue. Timing: 30–90 days.
   - Pricing / packaging adjustments for new deals: increases ARPU; more structural benefit. Timing: 60–120 days.
   - Sales acceleration (commissions reallocation to front-loaded deals): quick revenue but may cost short-term. Timing: 30–90 days.

2) Cost reductions (fast to medium, predictable)
   - Hiring freeze + pause open requisitions. Impact: ~immediate, depends on pipeline (saves full annualized hires if hired). Timing: immediate.
   - Reduce discretionary spend: travel, events, non-critical marketing. Impact: 5–15% of operating expense. Timing: immediate.
   - Vendor / SaaS renegotiation & consolidation: target largest subscriptions and negotiated discounts or annual prepay discounts. Impact: 5–10% of SaaS/ops cost. Timing: 30–60 days.
   - Contractors / consultants: reduce/triage non-critical contractors. Impact: 10–30% of payroll-like costs. Timing: 0–30 days.
   - Real estate / office optimization: move to smaller footprint or remote-first; depends on lease terms. Timing: 60–180 days.

3) Compensation & people levers (high impact, sensitive)
   - Freeze or reduce hiring, limit promotions.
   - Temporary salary reductions or deferred bonuses (with clear end/restore conditions). Impact: meaningful but morale risk. Timing: immediate (requires communication plan).
   - Targeted role reductions (if needed) — last resort. Timing: 30–60 days (legal/HR).

4) One-time / balance-sheet measures
   - Invoice factoring / sale of receivables: quick cash, cost of financing. Timing: immediate.
   - Delay discretionary CapEx / noncritical vendor payments (negotiate extended terms). Timing: immediate.
   - Monetize non-core assets / IP licensing. Timing: 30–120 days.

5) Financing options (backstop; dilutive or cost-bearing)
   - Bridge round / convertible note / SAFE: relatively fast, dilutive. Timing: 30–60 days.
   - Venture debt: non-dilutive, covenants, needs revenue traction; cost in interest/fees. Timing: 30–60 days.
   - Strategic partner investment or customer prepayments. Timing: 30–90 days.
   - Grants or non-dilutive government programs (if applicable). Timing: 60–180+ days.

C. Scenario math — how much runway you buy
Note: runway = cash / monthly net burn. Using cash = $1,200,000 and current burn = $62,000 → runway = 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (rounded: ~20 months).

Cost-cut scenarios (assume burn reduces proportionally)
- 10% reduction: burn = $55,800 → runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 20% reduction: burn = $49,600 → runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% reduction: burn = $43,400 → runway ≈ 27.6 months (+8.2 months)
- 50% reduction: burn = $31,000 → runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.3 months)

Revenue-lift scenarios (assume revenue improvements reduce net burn by the lift amount)
- +$20K/month revenue: burn = $42K → runway = 1,200,000 / 42,000 ≈ 28.6 months
- +$40K/month: burn = $22K → runway ≈ 54.5 months
- +$62K/month (breakeven): burn = $0 → company cash-neutral or cash-positive (runway extends indefinitely if positive)

Combined example:
- 20% cost cuts (burn → $49.6K) + +$20K/month revenue → net burn $29.6K → runway = 1,200,000 / 29,600 ≈ 40.5 months

Rule of thumb: a ~20% reduction in burn gets you just over 24 months runway; a ~45–50% reduction (or equivalent revenue lift) moves you into the 3+ year range.

D. Recommended plan, timeline & KPIs (practical, prioritized)
Immediate (0–30 days)
- Implement hiring freeze and pause nonessential spend. Owner: CEO/HR/Finance.
- Collections sprint: target DSO reduction and invoice factoring assessment. Owner: Finance/Sales.
- Identify top 5 vendors for renegotiation; cancel redundant SaaS. Owner: Finance/IT.
KPIs: monthly cash balance, DSO, committed vs discretionary spend, burn by major category.

Short term (30–60 days)
- Launch 30–60 day revenue sprints (upsell, renewals, enterprise outreach). Owner: Sales/Customer Success.
- Implement temporary compensation measures if needed (deferred bonuses). Owner: HR/Finance with legal counsel.
- Evaluate financing options (term sheet targets for venture debt/bridge). Owner: CFO.
KPIs: incremental monthly revenue from sprints, contract renewal rate, vendor savings realized.

Medium term (60–120 days)
- Reassess office footprint and longer-term restructuring if required.
- Finalize and execute financing if runway target still not met.
KPIs: runway in months, monthly burn trending, hire/attrition metrics.

E. Suggested target & ask (what to present to execs / board)
- Target: extend runway to at least 24 months immediately, with a stretch target of 36 months if market uncertainty increases.
- Proposed actions to hit 24 months: implement 20% cost reduction in non-core areas + 30-day collections sprint. If approved, present a 60-day savings tracker and ask for authority to negotiate vendor term changes and implement hiring freeze.
- If Board prefers less disruption: pursue a small bridge or venture debt to create optionality while revenue efforts scale.

F. Deck / slide structure (what to include)
1. Current position: cash, burn, runway, recent revenue trends (one slide)
2. Goal & target runway(s) (one slide)
3. Plan overview — three pillars (Revenue / Cost / Financing) (one slide)
4. Specific actions, owners & timeline (one slide)
5. Scenarios and runway math (one slide — show 10/20/30% savings & revenue lifts)
6. Ask & decision points (one slide)

If you want, I can:
- Draft the one-slide runway math and the finance narrative as a short slide or memo.
- Build a simple 3-scenario (base / conservative / aggressive) Excel-style model (I’ll need any more expense breakdowns you have — payroll, SaaS, contractors, marketing, rent — or I can make assumptions).
- Draft the email/board memo requesting approval for the 60-day plan.

Which of those would you like next? If you want the scenario model, share a quick breakdown of monthly expenses by major category (payroll, contractors, marketing, SaaS, rent/ops) or say “use assumptions” and I’ll make a clean model for the slides.

