I can help — below is a concise, directly usable set of analyses, quantified scenarios, prioritized actions, and ready-to-use finance narrative snippets you can drop into internal materials. First, a couple quick clarifying questions (so I tailor the numbers exactly): is the $62K/month burn your net cash burn (i.e., cash outflow after accounting for revenue), and is the $670K last-quarter revenue recurring/ARR-like or was it one-off? If yes to net burn, I’ll proceed assuming that throughout.

Key facts / quick math (assumes $62K is net burn)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Stated runway: ~20 months (exact = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months; rounded to 20)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter): 670,000 / 3 = $223,333 / month
- Implied monthly operating spend (if burn is net): expenses ≈ revenue + burn = 223,333 + 62,000 ≈ $285,333 / month

Scenario modeling — how cuts or raises change runway
(Using cash / new_monthly_burn = months runway)

A. Cost-reduction scenarios (percent cuts on current net burn of $62K)
- No change: 19.4 months
- 10% cut (burn = $55.8K): runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 20% cut (burn = $49.6K): runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% cut (burn = $43.4K): runway ≈ 27.7 months (+8.3 months)
- 50% cut (burn = $31.0K): runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.3 months)

B. Revenue / growth scenarios (if additional monthly revenue directly reduces net burn)
- Add $10K/month revenue => burn = 52K => runway ≈ 23.08 months (+3.7 months)
- Add $25K/month revenue => burn = 37K => runway ≈ 32.43 months (+13.1 months)
- Add $50K/month revenue => burn = 12K => runway ≈ 100 months (i.e., effectively cash-positive long-term)

C. Fundraising / financing to extend runway (at current burn $62K)
- Additional cash required to add X months = X * $62K
  - Add 6 months: $372K
  - Add 12 months: $744K
  - Add 18 months: $1.116M

Rule-of-thumb: every $1K/month reduction in burn buys roughly 1.2M*(1/(62K-1K) - 1/62K) ≈ 0.03–0.04 months extra; simpler to use the scenarios above for planning.

Concrete, prioritized runway-extension & cost-reduction options
(organized by speed of impact and trade-off)

1) Immediate / low-friction (implement within 0–30 days)
- Hiring freeze + pause all open searches (fast, minimal morale damage if communicated as temporary).
- Freeze non-essential discretionary spend (travel, events, conferences).
- Pause non-critical projects and capital expenditures.
- Tighten vendor T&Cs: ask for 30–60 day payment term extensions, discounts for prepayment, or temporary rate reductions.
- Collections acceleration: enforce faster invoicing, shorter payment terms, offer small early-payment discounts.
Impact: can usually save 5–15% of monthly spend quickly.

2) Short-term / moderate impact (30–90 days)
- Reduce contractor/consulting spend; transition to part-time or project-based work.
- Consolidate SaaS and subscriptions; eliminate duplicates and negotiate enterprise discounts.
- Temporary compensation actions (reduced hours, reduced salaries at senior levels, or short-term furloughs) — clear communication and end-dates are crucial.
- Real estate / facilities: renegotiate lease terms, sublet unused space, move to more flexible workspace.
Impact: 10–30% of monthly spend possible depending on choices and team structure.

3) Revenue acceleration actions (30–120 days)
- Offer short-term, high-discount prepay deals to enterprise customers to pull forward revenue.
- Focus sales on highest-conversion high-value segments; incentivize quick-close deals.
- Cross-sell and upsell to existing customers (cheaper and faster than new logo acquisition).
- Launch limited-time pricing changes or packaging that increases average contract value (with careful churn risk evaluation).
Impact: can materially reduce net burn if you can pull revenue forward or increase ARR; depends on sales cycle.

4) Financing & structural moves (45–120+ days)
- Raise an extension / bridge round (SAFE, convertible note, priced bridge) from existing investors or new angels.
- Venture debt or a revenue-based financing facility (faster than equity, but has covenants/repayment).
- Strategic partnership or customer prepayment agreements.
- Asset sales or licensing of IP where feasible.
Impact: immediate cash bolstering but creates dilution or repayment obligations; often required if cuts alone don’t reach target runway.

5) Medium-term structural cost lowers (3–6 months)
- Restructure teams for efficiency (merge roles, eliminate duplicative functions).
- Rebuild go-to-market with a higher-ROI acquisition funnel (offset marketing spend with higher conversion tactics).
- Outsource non-core functions to lower-cost providers.

Target-setting examples
- If goal = 24 months runway: target monthly burn = 1,200,000 / 24 = $50,000 → need to reduce burn by ~$12K / month (≈19% cut).
- If goal = 30 months runway: target burn = $40,000 → need to reduce by ~$22K / month (≈35% cut).
- If goal = add 12 months via fundraising: raise ~$744K.

Recommended step-by-step action plan (first 30 / 60 / 90 days)
- Week 0–1: Confirm definitions (net burn vs gross), finalize top-line forecast and cashflow model; identify discretionary spends >$10K.
- Week 1–2: Implement hiring freeze; pause non-essential projects; start vendor negotiations; roll out collections acceleration.
- Week 2–4: Present board/leadership with 2–3 scenarios (base, cut-20%, raise-$X) with quantified runway and impacts; seek approvals on preferred mix.
- Month 1–3: Execute headcount or contractor changes if needed; pursue fundraising or debt discussions; implement revenue acceleration plays.
- Month 3+: Re-assess, update model monthly, prepare for next raise threshold or return to growth hiring as runway stabilizes.

Finance narrative — short executive summary (use in board memo / leadership slides)
Option A — conservative (mix of cuts + small bridge)
“Executive summary: Stellar Labs holds $1.2M in cash and is running at a net cash burn of ~$62K/month, providing roughly 19–20 months of runway at current levels. To preserve optionality while protecting growth initiatives, we recommend a balanced approach: implement an immediate 15–20% reduction in discretionary and contractor spend (target ~ $9–12K/month savings), accelerate collections and near-term sales conversions, and pursue a $750K bridge to cover the next 12 months. This mix extends runway to ~30 months while minimizing disruptive personnel measures. We estimate the plan can be implemented within 60 days and will be tracked by monthly cash forecasts and weekly collections metrics.”

Option B — aggressive (cost-first)
“Executive summary: With $1.2M cash and current net burn of ~$62K/month (~19 months runway), we propose an aggressive cost reduction program to reach a 24–30 month runway without additional dilution. Target actions include a hiring freeze, 20–30% reduction in contractor and discretionary spending, and temporary compensation adjustments at the executive level. This will buy an additional 4–13 months of runway depending on depth of cuts. We will run this as a 90-day program with pre-defined KPIs and a re-investment plan if revenue accelerates.”

Internal all-hands / manager-level messaging (short)
“Current state: we have a healthy starting position with $1.2M on the balance sheet and a runway near 20 months. Our priorities are to protect the core product roadmap and customer success while improving operational efficiency. Over the next 30 days we will institute a temporary hiring freeze, reduce discretionary spend, and accelerate billing/collections. Where needed, we will implement short-term, targeted cost actions, and will be transparent about any changes. This is intended to buy us time to execute growth initiatives with lower risk of disruption.”

KPIs and reporting to include in the pack
- Weekly cash balance
- Monthly burn (actual and variance vs plan)
- Revenue recognized and cash collected (DSO)
- Pipeline and expected near-term bookings (30/60/90 day)
- Savings realized vs target for each cost bucket
- Progress on fundraising / financing conversations (status / timing / term sheet)

Risks and trade-offs (to present honestly)
- Deep cuts to R&D or GTM harm growth trajectory and future valuation.
- Salary reductions or furloughs can hurt morale and productivity if not timeboxed and clearly communicated.
- Raising debt increases fixed obligations; raising equity dilutes stakeholders — both have trade-offs vs the certainty of runway extension.

Next steps I can take for you
- Build a 3-scenario financial model (base, cost-reduction, fundraising) with monthly P&L, cashflow, and headcount schedule — I can draft that.
- Draft slides for a board update or an internal all-hands using the narratives above.
- Draft an email/board memo recommending a specific mix (e.g., 20% cost cut + $750K bridge) with supporting appendices.

Tell me:
