- Breakeven revenue lift required = current net burn / current monthly revenue = 62,000 / 223,333 ≈ 27.8% — so ~28% recurring revenue increase (or an equivalent combination of revenue and expense savings) gets you to break‑even.

Scenario math (simple and transparent)
(Formula used: runway = cash / net monthly burn)

If you reduce net monthly burn by X% (i.e., lower the current $62k by X%):
- -10% burn => new burn = $55,800 → runway ≈ 21.5 months
- -20% burn => new burn = $49,600 → runway ≈ 24.2 months
- -30% burn => new burn = $43,400 → runway ≈ 27.6 months
- -50% burn => new burn = $31,000 → runway ≈ 38.7 months

If you increase monthly revenue (current ≈ $223k) and hold costs constant:
- +10% revenue (+$22.3k) → new burn = $62k − $22.3k = $39.7k → runway ≈ 30.3 months
- +20% revenue (+$44.7k) → new burn = $17.3k → runway ≈ 69.2 months
- ~+28% revenue → roughly breakeven

Combined example (realistic mixed approach)
- Cut net burn by 20% (save ≈ $12.4k) AND grow revenue by 10% (≈ $22.3k) → new net burn ≈ $62k − $12.4k − $22.3k ≈ $27.3k → runway ≈ 44 months.

Levers to extend runway — organized and prioritized
Immediate (30 days) — quick wins, low execution risk
- Hiring freeze + pause open reqs (immediate savings; highest impact over 30–90 days)
- Pause discretionary spend: travel, conferences, events, noncritical marketing campaigns
- Cut or pause non‑essential vendor subscriptions; audit SaaS and license usage
- Immediately renegotiate payment terms with large vendors and landlords where possible
- Tighten receivables: accelerate invoicing, offer small discount for upfront/prepaid annual contracts

Near term (30–90 days) — moderate impact, needs negotiation
- Contractor/consultant re-scoping or reduction (convert to time‑boxed projects)
- Cloud and infrastructure cost optimization (rightsizing, reserved instances)
- Renegotiate sales commission clawbacks or temporarily shift to lower fixed + higher variable comp
- Marketing spend reallocation to higher‑ROI channels, pause lower-performing campaigns

Medium term (90–180 days) — strategic but higher friction
- Product/portfolio rationalization — sunset low-margin or non‑strategic products
- Consolidate offices or shift to a smaller footprint / remote-first to cut real estate
- Restructure vendor contracts, payment plans, or financing for capital items

As last resort (use cautiously; communicate transparently)
- Headcount reduction / layoffs (big impact but high cost and risk)
- Temporary salary reductions or executive pay deferrals
- Cancel or delay large capital or R&D projects

Revenue acceleration levers (often higher ROI than pure cuts)
- Push for annual upfront payments with discounts (improves cash now)
