Quick summary + one clarifying question
- Summary of what you gave: Stellar Labs — cash $1.2M, stated runway ~20 months, net burn $62K/month, revenue last quarter $670K (≈ $223K/month).
- Quick question: Is the $62K/month burn your net burn (cash outflow after counting revenue) or a gross burn (total cash outflow before revenue)? I’ll assume net burn for the calculations below — I’ll note where that assumption matters.

1) Current baseline math (assumes net burn = $62K/month)
- Current runway = $1,200,000 / $62,000 = 19.35 months (≈ 19–20 months). This matches the ~20 months you noted.

2) Runway impact — simple scenarios (assumes net burn)
- Reduce net burn by 10% (to $55.8K): runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- Reduce net burn by 20% (to $49.6K): runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- Reduce net burn by 30% (to $43.4K): runway ≈ 27.6 months (+8.3 months)
- Raise additional cash (adds runway = raise / $62K per month):
  - $500K -> +8.1 months
  - $1.0M -> +16.1 months
  - $2.0M -> +32.3 months

3) Revenue-growth impact (high-level)
- Quarterly revenue $670K → monthly ≈ $223K.
- If additional revenue dollars flow to the bottom line dollar-for-dollar (i.e., incremental gross margin ≈ 100%), then:
  - +10% revenue (≈ +$22.3K/month) lowers net burn to $39.7K → runway ≈ 30.3 months (+11 months)
  - +25% revenue (≈ +$55.8K/month) would nearly eliminate net burn (62K − 55.8K ≈ $6.2K) → runway ≈ 194 months
- Important caveat: revenue doesn’t usually translate 1:1 to net cash. Use contribution margin on incremental revenue (e.g., if incremental gross margin is 60%, then only 60% of revenue increase reduces net burn). I can run these with a margin assumption if you want.

4) Practical runway-extension levers (priority + estimated timing to realize)
A. Fast, low-friction (0–30 days)
- Hiring freeze and pause open reqs: immediate, ~5–10% payroll saving over next month(s) if hiring planned.
- Freeze discretionary spend (travel, events, conferences): immediate small savings.
- Vendor & SaaS renegotiation: immediate notices; savings 5–25% depending on contract flexibility.
- Accounts receivable acceleration (incentivize net-30 customers to prepay/discount for quicker payment): immediate cash, depends on customer base.
- Delay non-critical capital spend: immediate.

B. Medium (30–90 days)
- renegotiate leases/office footprint or shift to remote/hybrid — up to 10–30% of occupancy costs.
- Review contractors/consultants and convert to part-time or halt: possible 10–40% on contractor line.
- Implement targeted temporary salary reductions or voluntary unpaid leave programs: can produce material savings but require employee communications.

C. Structural / higher-impact (60–180 days)
- Rightsize headcount (targeted layoffs) — highest one-time cost but largest recurring savings. Example: reducing payroll 10–25% could cut monthly burn by similar percent (largest lever).
- Product roadmap triage: pause low ROI projects to reduce R&D spend.
- Outsource or consolidate functions (finance, HR) — moderate recurring savings.

Estimated savings (very rough guidance)
- Small optimizations (vendors, travel, hiring freeze): 5–15% of net burn → extends runway ~1–3 months.
- Deeper measures (contractor cuts + SaaS + small headcount adjustments): 20–35% → extends runway ~4–8 months.
- Major headcount reduction (10–25% of payroll depending on company mix): 30–50% → extends runway materially (8–20+ months depending on scale).

5) Financing & non-dilutive options
- Equity raise: straightforward but dilutive; high runway per $ raised ($1M → ~16 months).
- Venture debt / convertible note: faster, less dilution, covenants risk; typically adds 12–24 months depending on amount.
- Revenue-based financing: non-dilutive but expensive cost of capital; good if revenue growth is predictable.
- Customer prepayments / annual contracts with discount: immediate working capital.
- Grants, R&D credits, tax refunds: non-dilutive, lower amounts, application lead time.
- Receivables factoring: quick cash for invoices, with fee.

6) Tradeoffs and risks (how to present)
- Cost cuts: preserve runway but can slow growth, morale, and time-to-market.
- Fundraising: extends runway but may dilute or impose covenants; may take 60–120 days to close.
- Revenue growth focus: best long-term but slower to realize and requires investment (sales, marketing).
- Combination approach recommended: short-term cost controls + aggressive revenue pushes + targeted raise.

7) Recommended prioritized action plan (30/60/90 day)
- Immediate (0–7 days)
  - Confirm net vs gross burn and breakout by category (payroll, COGS, marketing, G&A).
  - Implement hiring freeze and pause discretionary spend.
  - Prepare communications: board update + all-hands draft (transparent, framed around choices).
- 7–30 days
  - Negotiate vendor contracts and collect receivables acceleration options.
  - Reforecast rolling 12-month cash model weekly; run scenarios (base, +10% rev, −20% cost, raise $X).
  - Begin outreach to debt and equity investors (soft market check).
- 30–90 days
  - Execute deeper cost actions per board-approved plan (if needed).
  - Close on financing or secure bridge if required.
  - Launch targeted revenue plays (enterprise upsell, pricing changes, pilot customer prepayments).

8) Finance narrative — short draft you can use in a slide or email
(Top-line, objective tone)
"Current position: Stellar Labs holds $1.2M in cash, with a current net cash burn of $62K/month, giving us ~19–20 months of runway at current performance. Last quarter revenue was $670K (≈ $223K/month), which provides a strong foundation.

Objective: Extend runway and de-risk the company while preserving growth options. Our preferred approach is a three‑pronged plan: (1) immediate cost discipline (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiations, pause discretionary spend), (2) focused revenue acceleration (enterprise upsells, annual prepayments, pricing optimization), and (3) targeted finance options (short bridge or revenue financing + larger equity round as needed). Together these actions can extend runway materially within 30–90 days without unnecessarily sacrificing growth."

9) Suggested KPIs & reporting (what the board will want)
- Monthly cash balance; monthly net burn; runway (months)
- Revenue M/M and Q/Q; ARR / forward 12 months revenue
- Contribution margin on incremental revenue
- Customer churn, CAC payback
- Progress on savings (actual vs target) and fundraising status

10) Next steps I can help with right away
- If you want, I can:
  - Draft the board slide deck / one-page summary + talking points (I can produce a 3–5 slide outline).
  - Build a 12-month cash-flow model with scenario toggles (give me the P&L breakout or I can work with assumptions).
  - Draft the employee communication / FAQ for cost actions.
  - Draft outreach email to potential lenders/investors.

Which of the above would be most useful first? Also confirm whether the $62K is net burn (after revenue) or gross so I can refine the numbers and contribution‑margin assumptions.

