Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a great starting set of numbers. I’ll (1) show the math and key assumptions, (2) model quick “what-if” scenarios (cost cuts and revenue changes) so you can see runway impact, (3) list practical runway-extension and cost-reduction levers (with estimated savings ranges and implementation timelines), and (4) give a tight finance narrative you can drop into internal materials (slides or a memo). If you want, I can also draft slides or the board memo once you confirm a few details.

1) Assumptions / baseline (what I used)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Stated runway: ~20 months (we’ll check math)
- Net burn: $62,000 / month (assumed net burn = cash outflow minus cash inflows)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → average monthly revenue = $670k / 3 = $223,333
- Implied total monthly expenses = revenue + net burn = 223,333 + 62,000 = 285,333

Check: runway = cash / net burn = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months → rounds to ~19–20 months (matches your number).

2) Quick scenario math (how savings change runway)
Notes: I apply % cuts to total monthly expenses (285,333). New burn = new expenses − current monthly revenue (223,333). Runway = 1,200,000 / new burn (if new burn > 0). If new burn ≤ 0 you are at break-even or net positive cash flow.

- Baseline (no change)
  - Monthly burn = $62,000 → runway ≈ 19.4 months

- 10% expense reduction
  - New expenses = 285,333 × 0.90 = 256,800
  - New burn = 256,800 − 223,333 = $33,467 / month
  - Runway ≈ 1,200,000 / 33,467 = 35.9 months

- 20% expense reduction
  - New expenses = 228,266
  - New burn = 228,266 − 223,333 = $4,933 / month
  - Runway ≈ 243 months (~20 years) — effectively near break-even (small net burn)

- Break-even cut required
  - Break-even happens when expenses = revenue
  - Required reduction = 1 − (revenue / expenses) = 1 − (223,333 / 285,333) ≈ 21.7%
  - So a ~21.7% cut to total expenses would move Stellar Labs to ~zero net burn.

- Aggressive (25% cut)
  - New expenses = 213,999 → net cash-positive ≈ $9,334 / month (you'd be growing cash)

3) Sensitivity: revenue declines matter
If revenue drops (common risk), runway shortens quickly:

Example: 20% revenue drop → monthly revenue = 178,667
- Current burn = 285,333 − 178,667 = $106,666 → runway = 1.2M / 106,666 = 11.25 months
- With a 10% expense cut simultaneous: new expenses 256,800 → new burn = 78,133 → runway ≈ 15.4 months

Takeaway: protect revenue lines and prioritize actions that both reduce expense and protect/grow revenue.

4) Practical levers to extend runway (prioritized, with rough expected savings & timing)
Immediate (0–90 days) — high impact, low friction
- Hiring freeze + pause open reqs: saves 100% of future hires; immediate effect. (Savings: depends on hiring plan; often 5–15% of expenses)
- Delay non-critical capital spend & projects: immediate; saves 1–5% quickly
- Reduce discretionary spend (travel, events, conferences): 2–5% savings
- Negotiate vendor terms / extend payment terms: improves short-term cash (cash flow, not necessarily expense)
- Accelerate collections / tighten DSO (incentives for early payment, enforce invoice terms) — near-term cash boost
- Convert monthly customers to prepay / offer discounts for annual prepayment: short-term cash inflow

Medium (1–6 months) — strategic but quicker to implement than long-term structural
- Reduce contractor / consultant spend; migrate to essential scope only: 3–10% savings
- Trim marketing spend to high-ROI channels; reallocate to lower-cost growth tactics: 2–8%
- Cloud & SaaS optimization (rightsizing, reserved instances): 2–7%
- Office & real estate (sublease unused space, renegotiate lease) — meaningful but dependent on agreements

Structural / harder (2–6 months) — larger impact but more disruptive
- Workforce restructuring (furloughs, selective layoffs, voluntary sabbaticals, reduced hours): possible large savings; sensitivity high. (Savings: depends on payroll share; payroll commonly 40–60% of total expenses)
- Compensation adjustments (temporary pay cuts, reduced bonus pools, equity mix): preserves jobs but reduces cash outflow
- Business model / pricing changes (raise prices, change contract terms): can increase revenue and margin but may require sales engagement

Balance with revenue protection:
- Protect top customer-facing teams (sales, customer success) where spending protects revenue
- Prioritize product features that enable retention or upsell

Non-dilutive / financing options (cash bridge)
- Short-term bank lines or receivables financing (factoring) for immediate cash — usually expensive
- Vendor financing / extended payables
- Sale-leaseback, secondary sale of non-core assets
- Grants or strategic customer prepayments / deposits

Dilutive options (raise capital)
- Bridge round or extension — fastest if you already have investor interest
- Venture debt — preserves equity but requires covenants/repayment ability
- Strategic investments / partnerships

5) Quick decision matrix (what to act on first)
- Immediate (high ROI / low pain): hiring freeze, pause discretionary spend, collect receivables, vendor renegotiation, cloud/SaaS optimization
- Short-term (impactful, moderate effort): contractor reduction, targeted headcount adjustments, marketing reallocation, annual prepayments
- Medium-term (bigger, needs approval): permanent headcount changes, renegotiate real estate, pursue financing

6) Finance narrative (short, board-ready — use as slide or memo intro)
“Stellar Labs enters this quarter with $1.2M in cash and an estimated runway of roughly 19–20 months based on current net burn of $62K/month and last quarter revenue of $670K. We have stress-tested the model and can materially extend runway through a combination of targeted expense reductions and revenue acceleration. Importantly, a focused 10% reduction in operating expenses increases runway to ~36 months; a ~22% reduction puts us at break-even. Our recommended near-term plan is to (a) implement an immediate hiring freeze and cut discretionary spend, (b) accelerate collections and pursue customer prepayment programs, and (c) optimize vendor and cloud contracts. These steps are designed to buy time while we pursue higher-return initiatives (pricing, upsell and enterprise deals) and consider lower-cost financing if needed. We request approval for the immediate measures above and a weekly cash review cadence to monitor progress against KPIs.”

7) Metrics & governance (what to report and cadence)
- Weekly: cash balance, net burn, runway (days/months), cash inflows (receipts), major vendor payments due
- Monthly: revenue by cohort, gross margin, operating expense by category, DSO, bookings/commits, hiring status
- Board: scenario updates (base, -10%, -20% costs; -20% revenue), progress on actions, ask vs. approvals

8) Next steps & offers
- I can: draft a one-page memo, a 3–5 slide deck for leadership/board, or a detailed model workbook that lets you toggle revenue and expense assumptions.
- Helpful confirmations so I can prepare concrete materials:
  - Is the $62K figure net burn (cash outflow after revenue)? Any nuance (one-time items)?
  - Is the $670K last-quarter revenue a normal run-rate, or are there trends (up or down) we should model?
  - Approx % of expenses that are payroll vs. contractors vs. SaaS/vendors vs. marketing (or a breakdown if available)
  - Any priorities to protect (R&D, sales, customer success) or roles you won’t touch?

If you confirm those details, I’ll build a short slide set (numbers + recommended actions + expected runway outcomes) and a 1-page finance narrative you can share with the exec team or board. Which deliverable would you like first?

