Nice — I can help you build a clear, data-driven finance narrative and concrete runway-extension plan. Below I’ll (1) confirm assumptions, (2) give a short summary you can use in an executive slide, (3) show quantified scenarios (cuts and extensions), (4) list prioritized cost-reduction and revenue/financing options (immediate → mid → longer term) with likely impact and risk, and (5) provide a short recommended narrative + sample internal talking points and asks. Tell me if you want this turned into slides, an internal memo, or an all-hands script.

Assumptions (please confirm)
- Cash on hand = $1,200,000.
- Current burn = $62,000 / month (I’ll assume this is net cash burn after operating revenue; if it’s gross payroll+opex before revenue, say so and I’ll re-run).
- Runway = cash / burn ≈ 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (you rounded to 20 months).
- Revenue last quarter = $670,000 (≈ $223,000 / month). I won’t assume future growth unless you want scenario modeling for revenue ramp.

1) Executive summary (one slide / one-paragraph)
Stellar Labs enters this quarter with $1.2M in cash and ~20 months of runway at the current net burn of $62K/month. Revenue last quarter was $670K (annualized ~ $2.68M). To protect optionality and support strategic growth, we recommend a two-track plan: (A) immediate 10–20% cost discipline to extend runway and improve unit economics, and (B) pursue parallel non-dilutive financing and a targeted equity bridge to preserve runway through key milestones. With a conservative 20% cost reduction the runway increases by ~5 months and gives time to execute revenue acceleration or raise on improved terms.

2) Quantified runway scenarios (quick math)
Current baseline
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Burn: $62,000 / month
- Runway: ≈ 19.4 months (~20 months)

If we reduce net burn by:
- 10% (burn = $55,800): runway ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 20% (burn = $49,600): runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% (burn = $43,400): runway ≈ 27.6 months (+8.2 months)

If we raise immediate cash (examples)
- $1.0M equity or convertible note: runway roughly doubles (1,200,000 + 1,000,000) / 62,000 ≈ 35.5 months
- $500K nondilutive (grants, tax refund, receivables financing): runway ≈ (1,700,000 / 62,000) ≈ 27.4 months

If revenue accelerates (example)
- Additional $50K/month net revenue reduces net burn by $50K/month. If current burn is net-of-revenue, then the burn could turn positive; clarify if burn is net.

3) Prioritized cost-reduction levers (Immediate — 0–60 days)
(High impact, low to medium risk)
- Hiring freeze: stop external hires and freeze open reqs (saves immediately; biggest single lever).
- Pause discretionary spend: travel, events, non-critical marketing campaigns, new tools/subscriptions (quick wins).
- Vendor renegotiation: extend payment terms, ask discounts, push renewals (fast cash timing wins).
- Reduce contractor spend / convert to part-time: easier to cut quickly than headcount.
- Cloud and SaaS optimization: right-size instances, pause unused licenses — often 10–30% savings.
- Slow capital projects / postpone non-essential R&D features.

(Mid-impact / moderate risk)
- Temporary salary reductions or reduced workweeks (targeted, leadership-first).
- Trim marketing ROI-negative campaigns and reallocate to highest-performing channels.
- Consolidate tools / cancel overlapping subscriptions.

(Labor restructuring / higher risk)
- Strategic headcount reductions: only if necessary and targeted to low ROI roles. Model severance & morale effects.

4) Revenue / cash-raising levers (Immediate → 90 days)
(Non-dilutive)
- Accelerate collections / invoice factoring: shorten DSO, sell receivables.
- Apply for grants, R&D tax credits, local economic development funds.
- Customer prepayments or extended contracts with discounts (annual prepay).
- Strategic partnerships / co-selling agreements that bring upfront payments.

(Dilutive or debt)
- Bridge convertible note or VC extension: smaller raise to cover 9–12 months while preparing larger round.
- Venture debt or revenue-based financing (if revenue consistent).
- Equity round: position as growth / value-creating raise given revenue traction.

(Operational)
- Sales push for high-margin products, upsells, and retention campaigns (improve CAC payback).
- Price optimization on renewals and new contracts.

5) Quick estimates of impact and timing
- Hiring freeze + pause discretionary spend: likely 5–10% burn reduction in 30 days.
- Vendor renegotiation + SaaS/cloud optimization: additional 3–8% within 30–60 days.
- Targeted headcount adjustments (if needed): one-time severance cost, then recurring 10–20% burn reduction after 45–90 days.
- Non-dilutive grants/tax credits: approval cycles 1–6 months; can produce lump-sum cash (size varies).

6) Risk and people considerations
- Communications: prioritize transparent, consistent messaging — explain why measures required and how savings are being redeployed to growth priorities.
- Morale & retention: leadership should lead by example (deferred comp, skipped raises). Provide clarity on timelines and support for affected employees.
- Customer and product impact: preserve core product development and delivery to avoid revenue erosion.

7) Finance narrative structure (use in board slide, internal memo)
- Title slide: Current position and ask
  - Cash: $1.2M | Net burn: $62K/mo | Runway: ~20 months | Revenue (last Q): $670K
- Key insight: Healthy revenue base + modest runway but we should extend runway and reduce execution risk.
- Proposed plan (two tracks):
  1) Immediate discipline (30–60 days): hiring freeze, pause discretionary spend, renegotiate vendors — target 10–20% burn reduction.
  2) Parallel financing and revenue acceleration (60–120 days): apply for non-dilutive funding, accelerate receivables, prepare bridge/carry raise.
- Scenario table: baseline, 10%, 20% cost reduction, plus a $500K bridge.
- Ask: Approve immediate hiring freeze & vendor renegotiation authority; authorize Finance to pursue grants and a short bridge; target a decision on potential headcount adjustments only after scenario modeling by [date].
- KPIs & milestones: cash on hand weekly, burn (actual) weekly, bookings, DSO, runway, 3-month rolling forecast. Review cadence: weekly finance ops, monthly board update.

8) Sample 2–3 sentence internal announcement (leadership tone)
“Stellar Labs posted strong revenue last quarter ($670K), and we enter this quarter with $1.2M in cash and roughly 20 months of runway. To increase optionality and support our growth plans, we’re enacting a temporary hiring freeze and pausing discretionary spend while we pursue non-dilutive funding and accelerate receivables. These steps will protect our priorities and give us time to execute our sales acceleration plan — we’ll share more details in the weekly update.”

9) Recommended next steps (what I can do for you)
- Confirm whether burn is net of revenue. If not, I’ll re-run projections using gross expense + revenue.
- I can build:
  - A 1-page board-ready slide with the scenario table and recommended asks.
  - A two-page internal memo with the detailed action plan, owners, timing and KPIs.
  - An employee announcement (all-hands script and FAQ).
- If you want, provide headcount table (roles + monthly cost) and top 15 vendors + monthly spend and I’ll quantify exact savings scenarios and produce a 6–12 month cash forecast.

Which output would you like first — a board slide, the internal memo, or the employee announcement? And confirm whether burn is net of revenue.

