If you want, I can draft the slide deck or an internal memo next — or build a one‑page Excel model with editable assumptions.

A. Quick reconciliation (baseline)
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Stated net burn: $62,000 / month
- Runway (cash / burn) = 1,200,000 ÷ 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (your “20 months” is consistent — round to 20 for messaging)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → average revenue ≈ $670,000 ÷ 3 ≈ $223,333 / month
- Implied monthly operating expenses = revenue + net burn ≈ 223,333 + 62,000 ≈ $285,333 / month

B. Runway-extension scenarios (simple, transparent math you can show)
Use these for a slide showing “months added” from savings, revenue, or fundraising.

1) Impact of monthly cost reductions (assume revenue unchanged)
- Save $10k / month → new burn = 52k → runway = 1.2M ÷ 52k ≈ 23.1 months (+3.7 months)
- Save $20k / month → new burn = 42k → runway ≈ 28.6 months (+9.2 months)
- Save $30k / month → new burn = 32k → runway ≈ 37.5 months (+18.1 months)
- Save $62k / month → new burn = 0 → break-even (runway effectively extends indefinitely if sustained)

2) Impact of revenue increases (assume expenses unchanged)
- Additional revenue $10k / month → new burn = 52k → runway ≈ 23.1 months (+3.7)
- Additional revenue $30k / month → new burn = 32k → runway ≈ 37.5 months (+18.1)
- Additional revenue $62k / month → reach breakeven

3) Impact of one-off fundraising additions to cash (burn unchanged)
- Raise $500k → new cash = 1.7M → runway = 1.7M ÷ 62k ≈ 27.4 months (+8.4 months)
- Raise $1.0M → runway ≈ 35.4 months (+16.0 months)

4) Combined example (realistic approach)
- Example: cut $20k/month and raise $750k
  - New burn = 42k
  - New cash = 1.95M
  - New runway = 1.95M ÷ 42k ≈ 46.4 months
This shows how modest cuts + modest raise give a very large runway extension.

C. Specific levers to extend runway (organized, with ballpark monthly savings or revenue impact and risks)

1) Near-term (0–3 months) — high-impact, low-complexity
- Hiring freeze + pause open roles — immediate savings; typical impact 5–15% of payroll spend. Risk: capacity gaps.
- Pause or reduce non-essential marketing campaigns (CPL/ CAC review) — saves $5–30k/mo depending on scale. Risk: slows demand generation.
- Renegotiate vendor & SaaS contracts (annual licenses, seat counts) — one-time and ongoing savings $3–15k/mo.
- Move to remote / reduce office footprint or sublease space — saves $5–25k/mo depending on lease.
- Convert contractors to part-time or terminate noncritical contractors — saves $5–30k/mo. Risk: delivery delays.

2) Medium-term (3–9 months) — structural
- Reorganize product/engineering to prioritize revenue-driving projects — reallocates spend, can increase conversion/time-to-revenue.
- Incentivize sales with higher-variable comp, reduced base — can lift revenue but may cost more per dollar sold short-term.
- Consolidate or sunset low-value products/features — saves engineering & support costs.
- Implement rigorous SaaS/IT cost governance (rightsizing cloud infra) — $5–40k/mo possible at scale.

3) Revenue acceleration
- Short-term pricing promotions, upsell campaigns to existing customers — high ROI; incremental revenue is high-margin.
- Enterprise/partner deals with upfront payments — target a few larger contracts to materially reduce burn.
- New channels (channel partners, white-label) — takes time but can scale quickly.

4) Financing options (tradeoffs)
- Equity raise (angels / VC): preserves runway, but dilutive and takes time (6–12 weeks typical).
- Bridge note or convertible note: faster, possibly less dilution in near term.
- Revenue-based financing: non-dilutive but expensive (revenue share).
- Debt / line of credit: only if predictable revenue and good covenant terms.
- Non-dilutive grants/tax credits (R&D tax credits, government grants): small to medium cash boosts, can be worth pursuing in parallel.

D. Finance narrative and recommended messaging (single-page summary to leadership / board)
Lead with an objective, state the facts, propose a plan of action, quantify outcomes, list risks and asks.

Suggested structure (short bullets you can paste into a slide or memo):

- Objective: Secure a 30–36 month runway to reach [specific milestones: e.g., ARR target, product launch, strategic partnerships, or next raise].
- Current state (facts): Cash $1.2M; net burn $62k/mo; implied monthly expenses ≈ $285k; revenue last quarter $670k → ~223k/mo; runway ≈ 20 months.
- Strategy (three pillars):
  1) Conserve cash — implement immediate cost actions to save $20–30k/mo (hiring freeze, SaaS & vendor renegotiation, marketing reduction).
  2) Accelerate revenue — prioritize high-velocity upsell/enterprise deals to target an extra $20–40k/mo in recurring revenue (short-term push).
  3) Secure financing — pursue a ~$750k bridge or small equity round to create runway while executing operational changes.
- Quantified outcome (example): Combined $20k/mo cost reduction + $30k/mo revenue uplift + $750k raise → runway extends from ~20 months to ~46 months (see appendix model).
- Risks and mitigations: (1) Talent morale — mitigate with clear communication and temporary measures; (2) Revenue slowdown from reduced marketing — offset with targeted sales push; (3) Fundraising timing — start process now and run parallel grant/credit applications.
- Asks (for leadership/board): Approve hiring freeze and vendor renegotiation plan; authorize fundraising process to raise $500–1M; target $20–40k/month in cost savings within 60 days.

E. Recommended next steps (practical)
1) Approve immediate actions (0–30 days): hiring freeze, top 5 vendor renegotiations, marketing pause, contractor audit.
2) Build a simple 3-scenario model (Conservative / Base / Aggressive) that shows runway under: status quo, 20k savings, 20k savings + 30k revenue lift, and raising $500k.
3) Launch fundraising outreach (convertible note / bridge) while exploring grants/credit.
4) Weekly KPI tracking: cash balance, net burn, bookings, churn, CAC payback.

F. Deliverables I can prepare for you right away
- A one-page “finance update” slide (facts, strategy, scenarios, ask)
- A 3-scenario Excel runway model (editable assumptions)
- A draft internal memo to staff/leadership explaining the plan and trade-offs

Tell me which deliverable you want first and whether you want the math shown using your “20 months” rounded figure or the precise calculation (19.4 months). If you want the model or slides, I’ll need either:
- an expense breakdown (payroll vs. ops vs. marketing vs. G&A) OR
- permission to use reasonable defaults (I’ll document assumptions) so I can produce the model and a suggested slide deck.

