1) Quick check of the numbers
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Reported burn: $62,000 / month
  - Runway = cash / burn = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months ≈ 19–20 months (your “20 months” is the rounded figure).
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → average monthly revenue ≈ $223,333.

Note: I’m assuming the $62K/month is your net cash burn (i.e., cash outflows minus cash inflows). If instead $62K is gross operating expense before revenue, tell me and I’ll re-run the scenarios (the arithmetic and implications change).

2) Runway-extension scenarios (simple cash math)
These show how runway changes if you reduce net burn by different percentages (holding cash constant):

- Current: burn $62,000 → runway ≈ 19.4 months
- −10% burn (to $55,800) → runway ≈ 21.5 months
- −20% burn (to $49,600) → runway ≈ 24.2 months
- −25% burn (to $46,500) → runway ≈ 25.8 months
- −30% burn (to $43,400) → runway ≈ 27.7 months
- −40% burn (to $37,200) → runway ≈ 32.3 months
- −50% burn (to $31,000) → runway ≈ 38.7 months

Targeting a ~24-month runway (common target if planning a raise) requires cutting net burn from $62k → ~$50k/month (~19% reduction).

If you prefer to extend runway via revenue rather than cuts, the needed increase in contribution margin equals the gap in net burn (e.g., to reduce net burn by $12k you need an extra $12k of monthly contribution margin). How much top-line revenue that implies depends on your gross contribution margin (if you tell me margin %, I’ll compute required revenue growth precisely).

3) Levers to extend runway — prioritized, with typical impact & tradeoffs

Immediate (0–60 days) — low-friction, high-impact
- Hiring freeze and postpone open reqs — saving: depends on roles; each senior hire often = $10–30k/month fully loaded. Low revenue impact if non-customer-facing.
- Pause discretionary spend (events, travel, non-essential marketing campaigns) — $5–20k/month.
- Vendor renegotiations (licenses, SaaS, agencies) and swap to annual vs monthly where favorable — $5–30k one-time+/month.
- Cloud & infra optimizations (rightsizing, reserved instances) — $2–15k/month.
- Accounts payable timing (negotiate extended payment terms) — improves near-term cash, not long-term burn.

Short-to-medium (1–4 months) — structural
- Reduce variable marketing that has weak ROI; redirect to high-ROI channels — can save $10–40k/month but risks slowing new revenue if cuts hit acquisition.
- Outsource vs headcount for non-core functions — tradeoff between speed and long-term cost.
- Hiring reductions / reorganize teams and role consolidation — can materially lower run rate but impacts morale and capacity.
- Salary deferral or temporary pay reductions (with clear timeline & equity swaps) — medium impact; needs strong internal comms.

Medium-to-long (3–9 months) — higher-impact but higher cost to reverse
- Role layoffs / headcount reduction — biggest single-tail saving; high morale and execution cost.
- Office footprint reduction / sublease — one-time gain + lower monthly occupancy.
- Strategic product prioritization (sunset low-value features) — reduces development cost; avoids future spend.

Revenue / non-dilutive financing levers (simultaneous)
- Acceleration of sales: push for enterprise pre-pay/annual contracts, discounts for upfront payments — immediate cash; impact depends on sales pipeline.
- Increase enterprise AR collection: tighten terms, early-pay incentives.
- Launch short-term revenue programs: limited-time offers, add-on higher-margin services.
- Non-dilutive financing: revenue-based financing, factoring, bridge loans, government grants — preserves equity but has cost/fees.
- Strategic partnerships or channel deals for upfront commitments.

4) How to prioritize (principles)
- Protect growth channels that have positive ROI (don’t cut the levers that pay back quickly).
- Prefer one-time savings and timing shifts before structural headcount reductions.
- Preserve customer experience and product roadmap critical to revenue retention/growth.
- Sequence actions: low-friction immediate moves first; structural moves only if runway target isn’t reachable otherwise.

5) Suggested metrics and reporting
- Weekly cash balance and 12-week cash forecast (rolling).
- Monthly burn waterfall: revenue → gross margin → OpEx by category → net burn.
- KPIs to watch: MRR/ARR growth, churn, CAC payback, gross margin %, sales pipeline conversion and average deal size.
- Scenario dashboard: current plan, conservative (no growth), and aggressive (revenue acceleration) cases.

6) Short finance narrative (3–4 sentences) you can place in internal deck
“Stellar Labs enters the quarter with $1.2M in cash and an approximately 19–20 month runway at our current net burn of ~$62K/month. With revenue of $670K last quarter (~$223K/month) and solid customer traction, our near-term focus is twofold: preserve runway to maintain strategic optionality while prioritizing investments with demonstrable ROI. To reach a 24-month runway target we recommend a prioritized cost efficiency plan (targeting a ~19% reduction in net burn through hiring discipline, vendor renegotiations, and discretionary-spend cuts), paired with immediate revenue acceleration initiatives (upfront contract incentives and AR improvement).”

7) Recommended next steps (what I can do next)
- I can draft an internal slide (or email) summarizing the above with the specific numbers and suggested action plan.
- To make the scenarios precise, I need:
  1. Confirmation: is $62K/month net burn (post-revenue) or gross OpEx (pre-revenue)?
  2. Breakdown of monthly OpEx (payroll, marketing, G&A, infra, rent) — even top-line buckets and amounts are enough.
  3. Gross margin / contribution margin on incremental revenue (so I can model revenue-led runway extension).
  4. Flags for cost items you don’t want to cut (e.g., R&D, core GTM).
  5. Are you planning to raise within 6–12 months, or do you prefer to avoid fundraising now?

If you want, tell me the answers to 1–4 and I’ll produce a short slide-ready one-page plan with exact savings by bucket and 3 forecast scenarios (base, improve revenue, cost-cut), plus a ready-to-send internal email.

