B — Scenario math (showing how runway changes)
I’ll use E = current monthly expenses = $285,333 and R = monthly revenue = $223,333. Net burn B = E − R = $62,000.

Impact of expense reductions (percent reduction applies to total monthly expenses E)
- 10% cut in expenses:
  - Expense reduction = E * 10% = $28,533 saved
  - New net burn = B − 28,533 = $33,467/month
  - New runway = 1,200,000 / 33,467 ≈ 36 months
- 20% cut in expenses:
  - Saved = $57,067 → New net burn ≈ $4,933 → Runway ≈ 243 months (effectively near break-even)
- ~22% cut in expenses ≈ $62,773 saved → net burn ≈ 0 (cashflow neutral)
- 25% cut → net cash positive ≈ $9.3K/month inflow

Impact of revenue increases (percent increase applies to monthly revenue R)
- +10% revenue:
  - Extra = $22,333 → New net burn = B − 22,333 = $39,667 → Runway ≈ 30 months
- +25% revenue:
  - Extra = $55,833 → New net burn ≈ $6,167 → Runway ≈ 195 months
- ~28% revenue growth → company becomes cashflow positive

Combinations (example)
- 10% expense cut + 10% revenue growth:
  - New E = 256,800; New R = 245,667 → New burn ≈ 11,133 → Runway ≈ 108 months

Notes/caveats:
- These results depend entirely on how feasible a given percent cut or revenue increase is for your business. A 10% expense cut is very different in operational difficulty than a 25% cut.
- Confirm what’s included in “burn rate” (one-offs, capex, taxes, debt service). If you have upcoming large milestones (product launches, marketing campaigns), model them separately.

C — Specific runway-extension and cost-reduction options (prioritized, with likely time-to-impact)

Immediate / low-friction (30–60 days) — high ROI, low morale impact
- Hiring freeze + pause open requisitions (savings: immediate; often largest recurring saving).
- Freeze raises / discretionary bonuses (short-term savings; communicate timeline and plan).
- Cut or pause non-critical marketing programs and events; reallocate to highest ROI channels (savings depends on spend).
- Vendor renegotiations (SaaS, telecom, consulting): ask for discounts or pause unused seats (30–60 days).
- Tighten working capital: accelerate collections (policies for faster invoicing, discount for prepayment), extend AP days where possible.
- Pause capital projects and discretionary travel.

Tactical / moderate impact (60–120 days) — some operational disruption
- Reduce / convert contractors and consultants to deliverables-based or part-time contracts.
- Consolidate or cut low-margin product lines or features to lower operating complexity.
- Optimize cloud / infrastructure spend (rightsizing, reserved instances) — 2–3 months to realize.
- Renegotiate office leases or move to hybrid/remote model (savings depend on lease terms).

Structural / higher impact (3–6 months+) — may affect morale or operations
- Headcount reduction or redeployment (last resort; large impact on cash).
- Temporary salary reductions + equity compensation to preserve cash.
- Shift fixed costs to variable where possible (outsourcing, revenue-sharing agreements).
- Sell or monetize non-core assets (IP, unused equipment).

Revenue-acceleration levers (fastest to slowest)
- Offer discounts for annual prepayment (immediate cash inflow).
- Upsell/cross-sell existing customers (higher conversion than cold acquisition).
- Short-term promotional offers targeted at high-ACV customers.
- Enterprises: push to close and convert pilots into paid contracts; accelerate contracting.
- Longer-term: pricing changes, new product monetization.

Financing options (if you want runway extension without immediate deep cuts)
- Bridge round (angel / existing investors) — fastest but dilutive.
- Venture debt (non-dilutive-ish, requires revenue/profit runway and covenants).
- Strategic partnership or customer pre-pay/merchant financing.
- Convertible notes/SAFE for fast close.
