3. Authorize immediate actions: hiring freeze, vendor renegotiation, pause discretionary spend. (Owner: CEO + Heads; due: immediate)
4. Parallel: prepare a financing plan (target raise amount and preferred instrument) in case execution misses targets. (Owner: CFO; due: 30 days)
5. Weekly burn and KPI cadence: report burn, cash balance, AR days, top 5 cost lines. (Owner: Finance Ops; cadence: weekly)

E — Suggested finance narrative (short, executive-ready)
Executive summary paragraph for internal slides:
"Stellar Labs entered this quarter with $1.2M in cash and an average net cash burn of $62K/month, implying ~19–20 months of runway under current operating conditions. With quarterly revenue of $670K (≈$223K/month), total monthly expenses are ~ $285K. We recommend a staged program to extend runway while preserving growth: immediate low-friction actions (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiations, tighter working capital) aimed at a 10% reduction in operating expenses, combined with focused revenue acceleration (annual prepay offers and upsells). Modeling shows a 10% expense reduction alone could extend runway to ~36 months; paired with modest revenue growth, we can reach cashflow neutrality. We’ll run this program in parallel with a contingency financing plan to ensure flexibility."

Short internal talking points for leadership:
- Current runway ~19–20 months — healthy but we can and should extend it to increase optionality.
- Target immediate actions that maximize cash with minimal strategic harm (hiring freeze, pause non-essential projects).
- Finance will deliver a detailed 90-day plan with savings targets by cost category and progress tracked weekly.

F — Metrics to include in the slide deck / memo
- Cash balance (current and projected)
- Net burn (monthly)
- Runway (months)
- Monthly revenue and MRR/ARR run-rate
- Top 5 expense categories and expected savings
- AR days, AP days, SaaS subscriptions, headcount FTEs

G — Next steps I can take for you
