Good — this is a strong starting point. I’ll 1) check the math, 2) show simple, transparent scenarios for how different cost / revenue moves change runway, 3) list specific runway-extension and cost-reduction levers (with expected impact ranges and pros/cons), and 4) give a tight finance narrative / slide-text you can drop into internal materials. Before I go further I have two quick clarifying questions (below) — otherwise I’ll assume burn = net cash burn (cash outflow after revenue).

Quick numbers check (based on what you gave)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Stated burn: $62,000 / month
- Runway = cash / burn = $1,200,000 / $62,000 = 19.35 months → this matches your “~20 months”
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → avg monthly revenue = $670k / 3 = $223,333
- Implied total monthly cash outflow = revenue + net burn = $223,333 + $62,000 = $285,333

Breakeven math (useful anchor)
- To hit net burn = $0 with current cost structure you need monthly revenue = monthly outflow = $285,333.
- That implies revenue must increase from $223,333 → $285,333 = +$62,000 / +27.8%.
- Alternatively, if revenue stays flat, you must cut monthly outflow by $62,000 (21.7% of current outflow) to reach breakeven.

Scenario modeling (straightforward sensitivity)
(Assume burn = net cash burn. Runway = 1.2M / net burn)

- Baseline: net burn = $62k → runway ≈ 19.4 months
- Reduce net burn by 20% (to $49.6k): runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- Reduce net burn by 30% (to $43.4k): runway ≈ 27.7 months (+8.3 months)
- Increase revenue by 20% (revenue → $268k/mo) with no cost change:
  - New net burn = outflow - new revenue = 285.3k - 268k = $17.3k → runway ≈ 69.2 months (turns situation into long runway)
- Combined modest moves (10% revenue growth + 10% cost reduction):
  - New revenue = 245.6k; new outflow = 285.3k * 0.9 = 256.8k → net burn = 11.2k → runway ≈ 107.7 months

Notes on those scenarios
- The dramatic runway improvements from modest revenue growth show revenue effectiveness: your revenue is already a large portion of spend, so small increases materially reduce net burn.
- These calculations assume burn is net after revenue; if your “burn” is defined differently internally, we should re-run with those definitions.

Concrete runway-extension options and estimated impact
(organized by speed & reversibility)

Immediate / 30-day (fast, low friction)
- Hiring freeze + pause open requisitions — expected savings: 2–8% of outflow depending on hiring plans. Fast, reversible.
- Pause non-critical contractor / consulting spend — expected savings: 2–6%. Quick to implement.
- Reduce discretionary spend (travel, events, conferences) — expected savings: 1–3%. Immediate.
- Aggressively collect AR, shorten payment terms, offer small early-payment discounts — improves cash inflows; can reduce near-term burn.
- Renegotiate vendor contracts & SaaS licenses (consolidate tools) — 1–4% savings; some vendors will give short-term credits.

Near-term / 30–90 days (moderate impact)
- Targeted headcount reductions / role consolidation — 10–25% of payroll spend depending scope. Fast cash impact but high people cost.
- Re-scope or delay noncritical projects / capex — 3–8% savings; preserves core product work.
- Real estate optimization (sublet / downsize desks) — 3–10% recurring, but may have transition costs.
- Marketing/GT M efficiency changes: pause low-ROI channels and prioritize channels with faster payback — can increase revenue cadence and reduce wasted spend.

Strategic / medium-term (90–180+ days)
- Price increases and packaging changes (annual prepay discounts to accelerate cash) — can materially increase cash and ARR; depends on market elasticity.
- Push customers to annual billing (with small discount) or secure prepayments/retainers — accelerates cash collections.
- Pursue large enterprise contracts with upfront payments or milestone payments.
- Product-led pricing / monetization changes (add higher-margin offerings) — higher long-term impact but requires product work.

Financing / non-cost options
- Raise a small bridge/seed extension (SAFE / convertible note): fast, but dilutive.
- Venture debt: non-dilutive but requires covenants; useful if you have predictable revenue and clear runway extension need.
- Revenue-based financing (RBF): non-dilutive equity but higher cash flow pressure while repaying.
- Strategic partnership / customer advance payments or co-investment (e.g., pilot funding).
- Grants or non-dilutive government funding (take time to apply).

Which levers to prioritize (recommended mix)
- Immediate: hiring freeze, pause contractors, tighten AR collections, pause nonessential spend. Low friction, fast savings.
- Near-term: vendor renegotiations, pricing/packaging review to push annual prepayments, reallocate marketing to highest ROI channels.
- If leadership will accept headcount changes: targeted reductions in non-core teams (centered on lowest ROI hires) — fastest durable savings.
- Simultaneously evaluate financing options to eliminate fundraising time pressure.

Practical KPIs & controls to include in the materials
- Cash balance (weekly)
- Net burn (monthly)
- Runway (months)
- Monthly revenue (and growth %)
- AR days / collections
- Customer churn / new bookings
- Hiring pipeline & committed offers
- Real-time forecast (best / base / downside) for next 12 months

Recommended runway target and ask
- With 20 months today you’re in a relatively comfortable position vs many startups, but you should still aim to extend runway to 24–30 months if you want to avoid raising under pressure.
- Proposed ask for leadership materials: request approval to implement the immediate measures (hiring freeze, pause contractors, vendor renegotiation, AR acceleration) plus a 60–90 day plan to deliver a 15–30% net burn reduction and a simultaneous revenue acceleration program (pricing/annual deals). If leadership wants minimal headcount impact, emphasize revenue acceleration + vendor and discretionary cuts.

Short, draft finance narrative you can use in an internal slide or memo
(2–3 short paragraphs and a 1-line ask)

- Current position: “As of today we have $1.2M in cash, a net burn of ~$62k/month, and roughly 19–20 months of runway. Revenue in the last quarter was $670k (avg $223k/month). Our current cash runway gives us time, but we should take prudent steps to extend runway and reduce execution risk given market uncertainty.”
- Key insight: “Because revenue already represents a large portion of monthly outflow, modest revenue growth (~28%) would put us at breakeven; alternatively, ~21.7% reduction in total spend would also achieve breakeven. A combined approach (moderate cost reductions + revenue acceleration) gives the most reliable and least disruptive path to substantially extending runway.”
- Recommendation / ask: “We propose a 60–90 day cash-acceleration plan: (1) immediate hiring freeze and pause of non-essential contractors; (2) aggressive AR collections and push for annual/prepaid customer contracts; (3) vendor and SaaS renegotiations; (4) reallocation of marketing to fastest-payback channels; and (5) evaluate a small, short-term financing bridge if needed. Goal: reduce net burn by 15–30% and/or deliver >15% revenue uplift to extend runway toward 24–30 months. We request leadership approval to begin immediate actions and commit to weekly cash updates.”

Suggested slide structure
- Slide 1: Snapshot (Cash, Burn, Runway, Last Quarter Revenue)
- Slide 2: Breakeven math & sensitivity (how much revenue increase / cost reduction to reach breakeven)
- Slide 3: Short-term actions (30-day list + expected $/month impact ranges)
- Slide 4: Medium-term actions (90-day list + risks/costs)
- Slide 5: Financing options (pros/cons)
- Slide 6: Ask & next steps (approvals requested + timeline + KPIs)

Next steps / what I can do for you
- I can draft the exact slide content or one-page memo using the narrative above.
- I can build a 12-month forecast model (best / base / downside) showing cash balance monthly under the scenarios you prefer.
- I can prepare a slide with the scenario table (20% cut, 30% cut, 20% revenue growth, combined) containing the explicit numbers we outlined.

Quick clarifying questions before I draft slides / models
1. Is the stated $62k/month your net burn after revenue, or is that a gross expense number? (My suggestions assume it’s net burn.)
2. Is your revenue recurring (ARR-like) or more lumpy/project-based? That changes how feasible pushing to prepay/annual contracts is.
3. Do you have any constraints on staff reductions or an explicit “no layoffs” preference from leadership?
4. Target runway you want to present to the board (24 months? 30 months?), and your deadline for the materials?

Tell me which of the next steps you want (slide draft, one-pager memo, or a 12-month financial forecast), and answer the clarifying questions if you can — I’ll prepare the materials and include clean tables you can paste into a deck.

