Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Helpful — I can structure this so you can drop the numbers into a slide or memo for leadership/board. First I’ll summarize the baseline, then show concrete scenario math for runway extension, list cost-reduction levers and financing options (with pros/cons), give a prioritized 30/60/90-day action plan, and finish with a short finance narrative you can use in internal materials. Quick question at the end so I can tailor recommendations: is the $62K/month burn your net burn after revenue (i.e., cash outflow minus inflows)? If not, tell me and I’ll adjust the math.

Baseline (facts you gave)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Reported runway: ~20 months (1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months)
- Net burn: $62,000 /month (assumed net of revenue)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → ~223,333/month

Note on assumptions
- I’ll assume the $62K is net cash burn (so gross monthly spend ≈ revenue + net burn ≈ 223,333 + 62,000 = ~285,333/month). If burn is gross (not net), tell me and I’ll recalc.

Runway scenarios (simple, transparent math)
Runway = cash / net burn.

A. Pure cost reduction (no revenue change)
- -10% burn (to $55,800): runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 ≈ 21.5 months (+2.2 months)
- -25% burn (to $46,500): runway ≈ 25.8 months (+6.5 months)
- -50% burn (to $31,000): runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.4 months)

B. Revenue growth (spending constant; revenue reduces net burn)
Current monthly revenue ≈ $223,333. If revenue grows by X%:
- +10% revenue => +22,333/mo revenue => new net burn = 62,000 - 22,333 = 39,667 → runway ≈ 30.3 months
- +25% revenue => +55,833 => new net burn ≈ 6,167 → runway ≈ 194.6 months (nearly break-even)
- +50% revenue => +111,667 => net cashflow becomes positive ≈ +49,667/mo (cash accretion)

C. Combined (example)
- Reduce burn 20% (to $49,600) AND increase revenue 10% (+22,333). New net burn = 49,600 - 22,333 = 27,267 → runway ≈ 44.0 months.

Takeaway: Modest revenue increases (10–25%) or moderate cost cuts (20–30%) materially extend runway. Hitting break-even or positive cash flow is most powerful.

Specific cost-reduction levers (by timing and impact)
Immediate (impact weeks; low complexity)
- Hiring freeze and pause all open requisitions.
- Freeze discretionary spend (travel, events, nonessential contractors).
- Pause new marketing campaigns until ROI is validated.
- Vendor renegotiation: renegotiate SaaS, cloud, and consulting rates; move to annual prepay discounts only if cash-positive.
- Reduce travel / office expenses; encourage remote-first where practical.

Near-term (30–60 days; moderate impact)
- Consolidate or cancel low-usage software licenses; rightsize cloud compute (commitment + autoscaling savings).
- Shift contractors to part-time or fixed-scope engagements.
- Reevaluate marketing funnel to stop low-ROI channels and double down on highest-converting channels (improve revenue per marketing $).
- Trim discretionary headcount where roles are duplicative; consider voluntary time-off and reduced hours programs.

Medium-term (60–120 days; higher impact)
- Strategic RIF if necessary (targeting roles after reviewing impact on growth and product roadmaps).
- Re-structure compensation: temporary salary reductions, deferred bonuses, equity mix adjustments (be mindful of morale and retention).
- Defer non-critical R&D projects; prioritize high-return roadmap items.

Non-headcount structural savings
- Renegotiate office lease or sublet unused space.
- Investigate vendor financing or extended payment terms (30→60/90 days).
- Apply for grants, R&D tax credits, and reimbursements where applicable.

Revenue acceleration ideas (fastest to implement prioritized)
- Offer annual prepayment discounts to customers to pull forward revenue.
- Increase upsells/expansion: targeted account success campaigns.
- Introduce or raise pricing on underpriced products or tiers (with a communication plan).
- Short-term promotional offers with strict margin controls to convert pipeline faster.
- Faster collections: reduce DSO by tightening payment terms, early-pay discounts.

Financing options (timeline, pros/cons)
- Bridge/convertible note (30–90 days): fast, less dilution in short term, but future conversion dilutive and adds complexity.
- Equity round (60–120+ days): increases runway but dilutive and time-consuming; good if you can demonstrate momentum.
- Venture debt (45–90 days): non-dilutive-ish, but requires covenants and revenue/AR collateral; interest + fees.
- Strategic partnership / prepayment from large customer: non-dilutive, can be fast if you have the right partner; may require discounts/commitments.
- Grants / government programs (timing variable): non-dilutive but usually small and takes admin work.
