Great — I can help. Below I’ll (1) confirm the current math, (2) show quick quantified scenarios for extending runway, (3) list prioritized cost-reduction and non-dilutive financing levers with realistic savings ranges and tradeoffs, and (4) give a short finance narrative / slide/email template you can use with leadership. Before I build a slide deck or an Excel model, I have a couple of quick questions (see bottom).

1) Baseline (confirmed)
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Burn: $62,000 / month (I’m assuming this is net burn = cash out minus cash in)
- Runway = cash / burn = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months → ~19–20 months (matches your 20 months)

If “burn” is not net (i.e., you mean only cash outflows), tell me and I’ll re-run the model.

2) Quick scenario math (how many months each action buys)
Assumptions: net burn decreases one-for-one with any permanent reduction in monthly net cash outflow or equivalent monthly net cash inflow increase.

- Current: 62,000 → 19.4 months
- 10% reduction in burn (to $55,800) → runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 = 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 20% reduction (to $49,600) → runway = 1,200,000 / 49,600 = 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% reduction (to $43,400) → runway = 1,200,000 / 43,400 = 27.7 months (+8.3 months)

Revenue-focused example (if net revenue/cash collection grows and reduces net burn)
- If monthly net revenue improves by $22,333 (which equals a 10% increase of your approx. monthly revenue of $223,333 — derived from $670K last quarter / 3), and that directly reduces net burn:
  - New net burn = 62,000 − 22,333 = 39,667 → runway = 1,200,000 / 39,667 = 30.3 months (+~11 months)
- Combine cost reduction + revenue lift: e.g., 20% cost cut (to $49,600) + $22k/mo revenue lift → net burn ≈ $27,267 → runway ≈ 44 months.

Note: those revenue scenarios assume collection and margins are similar and that revenue increases translate to cash immediately. If revenue is billed but collected later, effect is delayed.

3) Prioritized levers to extend runway (practical, ordered by speed / impact)
A. Immediate / quick wins (days → 1 month)
- Hiring freeze + stop open requisitions: immediate. Typical savings impact depends on hires planned; freeze preserves optional headcount.
- Pause non-essential marketing campaigns / events: 10–40k/mo possible depending on current spend.
- Cancel/trim underused SaaS subscriptions and vendor consolidation: 5–20k/mo.
- Delay contractor/consultant engagements or renegotiate scope: 10–50% of contractor spend.
- Collect receivables more aggressively / offer short-term discounts for prepayment: one-time cash inflow and improved working capital.

B. High-impact (2–6 weeks to implement)
- Targeted headcount reductions (last resort): a 10% headcount reduction often cuts total burn by ~6–20% depending on payroll weight.
- Temporary salary reductions (tiered by seniority) or voluntary reduced-hours/furloughs: preserves headcount but reduces monthly Opex.
- Renegotiate payment terms with vendors (extend payables) and landlords: improves near-term cash but may have relationship cost.
- Convert monthly customers to annual prepayment with discount: bring forward 3–12 months of cash.

C. Medium-term / funding-focused (1–3 months)
- Venture debt or convertible bridge: non-dilutive short-to-medium-term capital; quicker than equity but needs covenants and interest.
- Revenue-based financing / factoring for receivables: fast cash, but higher cost.
- Grants, tax credits, R&D credits: take time, but non-dilutive; can provide lump sums.
- Strategic partnerships or customer advance payments: negotiate pilot/PO with upfront payment.

D. Structural / long-term
- Product prioritization to improve gross margins (stop low-margin offerings).
- Outsourcing or offshoring specific engineering/ops functions where quality and speed allow.
- Office footprint reduction / remote-first policy: one-time costs but recurring savings.

Estimated impact guidance (very company-specific — I’ll refine if you share your Opex breakdown):
- Small operational trims (SaaS + marketing + discretionary) = 5–10% burn reduction typical.
- Hiring freeze + contractor cuts = additional 5–15%.
- One targeted reduction in payroll (10% headcount) = 6–20% depending on payroll share of Opex.
- Combined actions above can realistically achieve 20–30% burn reduction within 1–3 months if prioritized.

4) Financing options — pros/cons
- Venture debt: quick, non-dilutive; requires revenue/metrics and may include covenants / warrants; good for 6–18 months runway extension if you have predictable revenue.
- Revenue-based financing: tied to revenue %; flexible but expensive.
- Convertible bridge / equity: longer runway, dilutive, but less operational constraints.
- Customer prepayments/annual billings: low cost, immediate cash, but may reduce liquidity later.
- Grants / tax credits: free capital but uncertain and slow.

5) Finance narrative (short version you can use in a deck / leadership email)
Executive summary:
- “Stellar Labs enters Q2 with $1.2M cash and a current net burn of $62K/month, giving ~19–20 months of runway under current run-rate. That provides room to execute growth plans, but we should proactively extend runway to de-risk against market volatility and support strategic investments (product roadmap + key hires).”

Key drivers:
- Cash and runway figures (show math).
- Primary cost drivers (payroll, contractors, SaaS, marketing) — include % of spend if available.
- Revenue performance: last quarter revenue $670K — show collection timing and margin.

Recommended immediate actions (ask for approvals)
- Approve hiring freeze and pause discretionary marketing by EOM (projected 10–15% Opex reduction; estimated +2–4 months runway).
- Direct Finance to negotiate vendor deferrals and accelerate AR collections (target $X of upfront cash).
- Shortlist venture debt providers and initiate discussions (goal: secure $X non-dilutive facility to cover 12 months of planned runway gap).
- Contingency plan: if cash < $Y or runway < 12 months, implement more aggressive cost measures (target 20% total Opex reduction).

Scenarios to present in leadership meeting
- Base case (no changes): ~19–20 months runway.
- Moderate actions (freeze + 10% cuts + AR acceleration): ~24–26 months.
- Conservative plan (20% cost reduction + sales lift / prepayments or small debt): 36+ months.

KPIs and cadence
- Weekly cash tracker, 14-day AR aging update, monthly Opex vs plan, and a decision checkpoint every 30 days until runway threshold is extended to target (e.g., 24 months or CFO-approved threshold).

6) Suggested next steps (how I can help)
- I can build a one-page slide with these scenarios and the math.
- Or I can build a simple Excel scenario model where you can toggle % cost cuts, revenue lift, and new financing to see runway impact.
- To refine, I need:
  - Confirm: Is $62K/month net burn after revenue? (yes/no)
  - Monthly Opex breakdown (payroll, marketing, R&D, G&A, SaaS, contractors)
  - Average gross margin on revenue and AR collection terms (days sales outstanding)
  - Any constraints/preferences on fundraising (no dilution, max debt, timeline)
  - Target runway you want to present (e.g., 24 months)

If you give me those details I’ll produce either the slide deck language + 3 scenario charts or the Excel scenario model — whichever you prefer. Which would you like me to prepare next?

