B — Quick scenario math (all based on net-burn = $62K unless you tell me otherwise)

Baseline
- Cash $1.2M / $62K = 19.4 months runway (≈ 20 months)

If we reduce net burn by X%:
- 10% cut → burn = $55.8K → runway = 21.5 months (+2.1)
- 20% cut → burn = $49.6K → runway = 24.2 months (+4.8)
- 30% cut → burn = $43.4K → runway = 27.6 months (+8.2)
- 40% cut → burn = $37.2K → runway = 32.3 months (+12.9)
- 50% cut → burn = $31.0K → runway = 38.7 months (+19.3)

If instead we think in revenue-growth terms (using implied expense math)
- Implied monthly expense = revenue (223.3K) + net burn (62K) = ≈ $285.3K/month.
- Revenue up 10% (≈ +$22.3K/month) → new net burn ≈ $39.7K → runway ≈ 30.3 months
- Revenue up 25% (≈ +$55.8K/month) → new net burn ≈ $6.2K → runway ≈ 193.5 months
- Revenue up 50% (≈ +$111.7K/month) → net burn flips to positive cashflow ≈ +$49.7K/month (cash builds instead of burning)

Takeaway: Because revenue already covers much of monthly expenses, modest revenue gains or modest cuts in net burn materially extend runway — small moves can have outsized effects.

C — Prioritized cost-reduction levers (practical, impact / timeline)
Immediate (0–30 days) — low implementation time, quick impact
- Hiring freeze + pause open requisitions (saves full comp for role quickly).
- Freeze discretionary spend: travel, events, non-essential training.
- Pause non-critical vendor / SaaS subscriptions; consolidate overlapping tools. (Savings: 2–8% of Opex depending on tooling.)
- Cut or defer marketing spend with low ROI; focus spend only on high-CTRs and direct-response channels.
- Tighten T&E / corporate cards.

Short term (30–90 days) — needs approvals, moderate impact
- Renegotiate vendor contracts and payment terms (ask for discounts, extended payment terms, volume pricing).
- Move portions of fixed comp to variable (defer bonuses, shift to performance bonuses or commissions).
- Convert contractors to short-term consulting or reduce hours where possible.
- Reduce office footprint / sublet excess space or shift to hybrid remote-first policy.
- Cancel or defer non-critical hiring and freeze promotions.

Medium term (3–6 months) — structural, higher impact
- Rightsize teams where overlap exists (targeted layoffs only if necessary); do this with clear cost-to-impact analysis.
- Outsource non-core functions (finance ops, payroll, helpdesk) if cheaper than full-time hires.
- Reevaluate benefits vendor contracts, health plans, 401(k) admin costs.
- Revisit compensation mix long-term: equity vs cash, stipend adjustments.

Estimated impacts (very high-level ballpark — you'll want to map these to your P&L):
- SaaS / tools rationalization: 2–8% of opex
- Vendor renegotiation + payment timing: 1–4% cash flow improvement / near-term
- Hiring freeze + contractor reductions: 8–30% of payroll spend (biggest single lever)
- Facility savings: 3–8% of opex if footprint is trimmed
- Combined near-term achievable cut: 10–25% of net burn is realistic with modest pain; 30–50% requires deeper structural changes including layoffs.

D — Runway extension options (non-exhaustive) and tradeoffs

1) Non-dilutive / near-term cash
- Customer prepayments / annual prepay discounts (fast, no dilution; may reduce future ARR visibility)
- Accelerate collections / factoring of receivables (fast but may cost fees)
- Grants or government R&D credits (time to get, non-dilutive)
- Revenue-based financing (repay from revenue — no equity but high ongoing payments)

2) Debt / hybrid
- Venture debt (good if you have collateral or committed revenue; lower dilution but covenants and interest cost)
- Short-term bridge loans (quicker; interest + possible warrants)

3) Equity
- Raise new equity / priced round (dilution; can reset cash runway significantly)
- SAFE / convertible note bridge (faster than priced round; still dilutive later)

4) Strategic alternatives
- Strategic investor / corporate partnership (can bring sales + cash; may require concessions)
- M&A: sell non-core assets or business lines; or seek acquisition / strategic minority sale if timeline urgent

Tradeoffs:
- Non-dilutive options often cost more (fees/interest) or reduce future revenue (prepayments).
- Equity dilutes but may give more runway at lower ongoing cash cost.
- Debt requires disciplined forecasting and covenant compliance.

E — Finance narrative (short form + slide / email copy)

