Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Great — this is a strong starting position. Below I’ll (1) confirm the baseline math/assumptions, (2) show concrete runway-extension scenarios (cost cuts, revenue, fundraising), (3) list practical cost-reduction and revenue-acceleration options (with expected impact and risks), and (4) give a tight finance narrative + slide / memo structure you can drop into internal materials.

Baseline (assumptions and checks)
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Reported net burn: $62,000 / month (assumed to be net cash burn = expenses - revenue)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → monthly revenue ≈ $670,000 / 3 = $223,333
- Implied total monthly expenses = revenue + net burn = 223,333 + 62,000 ≈ $285,333 / month
- Current runway (cash / net burn) = 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (your “20 months” is the same within rounding)

Key observation: revenue (~$223k/mo) is substantial relative to expenses. Small changes to either revenue or expenses move net burn dramatically.

Scenario modeling (straightforward, transparent math)
I’ll present a few realistic scenarios — all assume revenue and expenses change only as specified in that scenario.

1) No change (status quo)
- Net burn = $62k/mo → runway ≈ 19.4 months

2) Targeted one-off savings (save $30k/month)
- New net burn = $62k − $30k = $32k/mo
- Runway = 1,200,000 / 32,000 = 37.5 months

3) 15% operating expense reduction (across the board)
- New expenses = 285,333 × 0.85 = 242,533
- Revenue stays = 223,333 → new net burn ≈ 19,200 /mo
- Runway = 1,200,000 / 19,200 ≈ 62.5 months

4) 25% operating expense reduction
- Expenses = 285,333 × 0.75 = 214,000
- Net burn = 214,000 − 223,333 = −9,333 → i.e., positive cash flow of ~$9.3k/mo (cash grows)
- Runway: cash increases each month, so runway becomes indefinite once this level is sustained

5) Revenue growth only: +20% revenue (no expense change)
- Revenue = 223,333 × 1.20 = 268,000
- Net burn = 285,333 − 268,000 = 17,333 /mo
- Runway = 1,200,000 / 17,333 ≈ 69.3 months

6) Combined modest actions: 10% revenue growth + 15% expense cut
- Revenue = 223,333 × 1.10 = 245,666
- Expenses = 285,333 × 0.85 = 242,533
- Net burn = 242,533 − 245,666 ≈ −3,133 → slightly positive cash flow

Fundraise sizing (simple targets at current net burn)
- To extend runway to 24 months at current burn: needed cash = 24 × 62k = 1,488,000 → gap = 1,488,000 − 1,200,000 = $288k
- To reach 36 months at current burn: needed cash = 36 × 62k = 2,232,000 → gap = $1,032,000

(If you plan to perform cost reduction in parallel, raise requirements fall quickly — e.g., reduce burn to $32k/mo, and current cash already supports ~37.5 months, so no raise required to hit 36 months.)

Cost-reduction levers (prioritized, practical)
Quick wins (30 days)
- Hiring freeze for open roles and pause nonessential contractors (saves quickly, minimal process) — impact depends on headcount but often 10–20% of payroll cost.
- Pause new marketing/paid campaigns with low ROI; reallocate to highest-ROI channels (saves immediate cash).
- Defer or renegotiate discretionary spend (conferences, office fit-out, travel).
- Negotiate vendor terms and payment schedules — push out payables, get discounts for upfront payments where helpful.

Medium-term (30–90 days)
- Renegotiate software/licenses and cloud spend — often 10–30% reducible with commitment changes.
- Reduce variable costs tied to usage; right-size cloud instances and reserved capacity.
- Evaluate underperforming projects / product lines and discontinue quickly.
- Sublease unused office space or move to smaller footprint.

Hard but effective (90+ days)
- Headcount reduction where necessary — highest savings but high morale/exec risk.
- Outsource high-cost functions or shift to contractors in lower-cost markets.
- Vendor consolidation and long-term renegotiations (volume discounts).

Revenue-acceleration levers (prioritized)
Immediate / near-term (30–90 days)
- Short sales sprints: prioritize highest-probability existing deals and accelerate closings with discounts for prepayments.
- Upsell/cross-sell targeted accounts — quick lift with low incremental cost.
- Introduce limited-time pricing incentives for annual prepayments (improves cash).
- Collections improvement: shorten DSO, pursuit of overdue AR, require partial deposits.

Strategic (90–180+ days)
- Price optimization for new customers and select legacy customers (carefully — test first).
- Enterprise sales push for larger contracts (longer cycle but materially larger ACV).
- Productized services or higher-margin packaged offerings.

Financing options (pros/cons)
- Small equity round (seed / bridge): non-dilutive to operations, slower, time-consuming; good if you want >$1M and to add runway without debt covenants.
- Venture debt: faster, less dilutive, but comes with covenants, interest, and requires evidence of revenue/growth. Good for revenue-generating businesses looking to extend runway 12–24 months.
- Convertible notes / SAFEs: fast and founder-friendly for short bridge.
- Strategic partnerships or prepayments from customers (revenue-like, non-dilutive).
- Bank line-of-credit or receivables financing to smooth working capital.

Practical recommended plan (actionable, prioritized)
1. Immediate (0–30 days)
   - Implement hiring freeze and pause low-ROI marketing: target $20–40k/month immediate savings.
   - Start vendor renegotiations and cloud-cost reduction: target additional $10–20k/month.
   - Tighten collections and offer discounts for prepayment: target accelerate ~$100k+ cash inflow if executed on large accounts.
   - Board/Leadership: present current runway math and ask for authority to implement these measures.

2. Near-term (30–90 days)
   - Execute 10–15% operating-cost reduction plan (across contractors, travel, software).
   - Launch focused sales push (enterprise deals, upsell campaign) to target +10% revenue in next 1–2 quarters.
   - If necessary, start term sheet conversations for ~ $500k–$1M bridge (venture debt or SAFE) as contingency if savings or revenue moves are delayed.

3. Medium-term (90–180 days)
   - Close permanent structural changes (headcount adjustments if still necessary).
   - Reinvest small portion of savings into highest ROI go-to-market initiatives.
   - Reforecast monthly with actuals; update board.

Risk/mitigation
- Revenue cuts from cost reductions (esp. marketing/hiring slowdowns): mitigate with tight A/B testing and redirect savings to highest-ROI channels.
- Morale impact from cuts: clear communication, transparent timeline, severance and support where applicable.
- Debt covenants: maintain conservative covenants and plan to pay down quickly if using venture debt.

Finance narrative (short, crisp — for internal materials / board)
Executive summary (2–3 sentences):
"Stellar Labs is cash-positive in the near-term with $1.2M in cash and a current net burn of $62k/month, giving ~19–20 months of runway. With targeted operational actions and a focused revenue acceleration plan, we can reliably extend runway to 36+ months without a material raise — or achieve >24 months with a modest bridge raise (~$300k). We propose a staged plan to (1) capture immediate savings, (2) accelerate high-probability revenue, and (3) keep financing options open as contingency."

Key supporting bullets:
- Baseline metrics: cash $1.2M; monthly revenue ~$223k; monthly expenses ~$285k; net burn $62k; runway ~19.4 months.
- Path A (cost-first): a 15% expense reduction reduces burn to ~$19k/mo and extends runway to ~62 months.
- Path B (revenue-first): 20% revenue growth reduces burn to ~$17k/mo and extends runway to ~69 months.
- Recommended balanced approach: quick cost saves + targeted revenue moves expected to reach break-even within 2–3 quarters and create a buffer for strategic investment.

Slide / one-pager structure to present
1. Title / Ask (what approval you need)
2. Current position & KPIs (cash, burn, runway, revenue)
3. Key assumptions (how burn is calculated)
4. Scenarios (baseline, conservative cuts, revenue-up, combined) + headline numbers
5. Recommended plan (Immediate / Near / Medium term) with owners & timelines
6. Financing options & ask (if you want to pursue)
7. Risks & mitigations
8. Appendix: detailed monthly math and sensitivity tables

KPIs to track weekly / monthly
- Cash balance (weekly)
- Net burn / month
- Runway (months)
- Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR) or Monthly Revenue
- DSO / AR aging
- CAC, LTV if applicable
- Cost savings realized (vs target)

Next steps I can take for you
- Draft the one-page finance memo or slide deck using the above structure (I can populate the scenario tables and charts).
- Build a short sensitivity table for additional % changes you want to test (I can run any combinations).
- Draft board talking points or an email seeking approval for the initial cost saves.

Which of the next steps would you like me to do now? If you want the memo/slides, tell me: (a) preferred runway target (24, 36 months, or break-even), and (b) appetite for headcount reductions vs fundraising (low/medium/high).

