If burn is net of revenue, that means operating cash outflows before revenue were materially higher and revenue helped reduce the net burn to $62k.

2) Scenario modeling — examples you can use in slides
(rounded numbers)

A. Cost reduction scenarios (no new capital)
- Current runway: ~19.4 months
- 10% cut to burn (new burn = $55.8k) → runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 20% cut (new burn = $49.6k) → runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% cut (new burn = $43.4k) → runway ≈ 27.7 months (+8.3 months)
- 35% cut (new burn ≈ $40.3k) → runway ≈ 29.8 months (~30 months)

B. Capital raise scenarios (no cost cuts)
- Raise $500k → cash = $1.7M → runway = 1,700,000 / 62,000 ≈ 27.4 months
- Raise $660k → cash = $1.86M → runway ≈ 30.0 months
- Raise $1.0M → cash = $2.2M → runway ≈ 35.5 months

C. Mixed approach examples
- Raise $300k + cut burn 15% (new burn ≈ $52.7k):
  cash = $1.5M → runway ≈ 1,500,000 / 52,700 ≈ 28.5 months
These show you can reach a 24–36 month horizon with combinations of modest raises and moderate cuts instead of drastic layoffs.

D. Revenue-improvement impact (illustrative)
- If you can increase monthly revenue by $30k (and costs remain constant), net burn would drop from 62k → 32k → runway = 1.2M / 32k ≈ 37.5 months.
- Realistic revenue levers can be faster to implement (annual prepayments, price increases, cross-sell) and have high ROI with low cash impact.

3) Cost reduction levers — prioritized with typical impact ranges
(Short-term — implement in 0–3 months)
- Hiring freeze / pause on open reqs: saves 2–8% of burn depending on hiring pipeline
- Pause or reduce contractor & consultant spend: 2–10%
- Freeze discretionary spend (events, travel, training): 1–4%
- Vendor renegotiation (SaaS, hosting, agency fees): 1–6%
- Delay or reprioritize non-critical projects: 1–8%

(Medium-term — 3–6 months)
- Reduce marketing spend or shift to high-ROI channels: 3–10%
- Office footprint optimization / remote-first policy: 1–5%
- Consolidate tools / eliminate duplicate services: 1–5%
- Implement performance-based hiring / replace FT cash comp with equity for some roles: 1–4%

(Longer-term / structural)
- Headcount reductions or reorganizations (if required): 10–30% of payroll cost depending on scope
- Product rationalization (sunset unprofitable lines): depends on revenue composition
- Move to lower fixed-cost model (outsourcing, variable capacity): variable effectiveness

Notes:
- Payroll is usually the largest single line — savings estimates depend on how many roles you can freeze or convert to performance/equity.
- Sequence: prefer non-permanent measures first (hiring freeze, contractor cuts, vendor renegotiations) before permanent headcount reductions.

4) Financing & working-capital options
(Quick wins)
- Accelerate collections: offer small discounts for early/annual prepayments; reduce DSO
- Ask large customers for prepayments or milestone payments
- Invoice factoring for short-term AR liquidity (costly but fast)

(Traditional financing)
- Bridge round or equity raise (seed / extension): $300k–$1M typical for shorter-term needs
- Venture debt or convertible note: preserves equity, but requires repayment / covenants
- Strategic investors or corporate partnerships (customer-funded pilots)

(Other)
- Government grants, R&D tax credits: one-time inflow but can be meaningful
- Sale/leaseback or monetizing non-core assets

