Key assumptions / clarification needed
- I assume the burn rate you quoted ($62K/mo) is net burn (cash outflow after operating cash inflows). That matches your stated ~20 months runway on $1.2M cash (1.2M / 62K ≈ 19.4 months). If instead $62K is gross spend (pre-revenue), the math and options change — please confirm.
- I don’t have expense line-item breakdown (payroll, contractors, marketing, rent, vendor spend), AR balance / DSO, or committed CAPEX. If you can share those, I’ll produce exact savings scenarios. Below I use formulas and example scenarios you can adapt.

Quick baseline
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Net burn: $62,000 / month
- Current runway: 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (≈ 20 months, your figure)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 (≈ $223K / month average) — good revenue relative to burn, so focus on improving conversion/collections and margin leverage.

How much runway you buy with different cost reductions
(Starting runway M = 19.35 months)

- 10% cost reduction → new burn = 0.9 * 62K = 55.8K → runway ≈ 21.5 months (+2.15 months)
- 20% cost reduction → new burn = 49.6K → runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- 30% cost reduction → new burn = 43.4K → runway ≈ 27.6 months (+8.3 months)
- 40% cost reduction → new burn = 37.2K → runway ≈ 32.3 months (+12.9 months)
- 50% cost reduction → new burn = 31K → runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.35 months)

Rule of thumb: because runway = cash / burn, a x% reduction in burn gives runway × 1/(1−x). Even modest savings meaningfully extend runway when starting runway is long.

Specific cost-reduction & runway-extension levers (with estimated impacts and pros/cons)
(You’ll need to map these to your P&L to get exact numbers.)

Immediate (0–30 days) — low-friction, fast impact
1. Hiring freeze + pause contractor spend
   - Impact: If payroll/contractors = 60% of burn, pausing hiring and contingency contractors could reduce overall burn by 10–20% quickly.
   - Pros: Minimal employee morale impact if framed as temporary; fast cash impact.
   - Cons: slows growth / product momentum.

2. Cut discretionary and marketing spend by 30–50%
   - Impact: Often 5–10% burn reduction depending on spend level.
   - Pros: Immediate saving, easy to reverse.
   - Cons: May slow new customer acquisition.

3. Tighten procurement / cancel unused SaaS, renegotiate vendor terms
   - Impact: 2–8% burn reduction.
   - Pros: Fast; often quick one-time savings and better payment terms.
   - Cons: Operational disruption if not managed.

4. Accelerate collections / shorten payment terms / offer prepay discounts
   - Impact: Improves cash conversion; reduces short-term cash needs.
   - Pros: Raises liquidity without spending or headcount changes.
   - Cons: May slightly reduce revenue margin (if discount used).

Short-term (30–90 days) — meaningful structural steps
5. Vendor renegotiation / extended payment terms
   - Impact: Improves cash runway without headcount cuts (one-time or ongoing).
   - Pros: Preserves operations; negotiable.
   - Cons: May strain vendor relationships.

6. Re-scope or pause lower-priority projects and product development
   - Impact: Ongoing burn reduction depending on project size (could be 5–20%).
   - Pros: Focuses resources on revenue-generating work.
   - Cons: Opportunity cost; product roadmap delay.

7. Temporary salary deferrals or reduced hours (with voluntary options)
   - Impact: If payroll is a large share, even a 10% temporary save = material.
   - Pros: Less disruptive than layoffs; preserves team.
   - Cons: Morale risk; must be legal/compliant.

Medium-term (90–180 days) — tougher decisions, bigger impact
8. Workforce reductions (targeted)
   - Impact: Direct reduction in burn proportional to payroll saved. Example: 15% headcount reduction reduces payroll by ~15% → maybe 9–12% total burn reduction.
   - Pros: Quick and durable savings.
   - Cons: Very high morale/brand impact; loss of capability.

9. Real estate consolidation or rent negotiation (sublease, termination)
   - Impact: One-time and ongoing savings depending on leases.
   - Pros: Large long-term savings in overhead.
   - Cons: May have termination fees.

10. Defer non-critical CAPEX and hire plans
   - Impact: Improves near-term cash without headcount change.
   - Pros: No layoffs; simple.
   - Cons: Delays growth investments.

Financing / revenue options (non-cut paths)
11. Small equity raise / bridge/convertible note
   - Need to extend to 24 months: cash required = 24*62K − 1.2M = 288K (i.e., raise ~$300K bridge to reach 24 months at current burn).
   - For 36 months: need ~1,032K.
   - Pros: Preserves operations and team; time to execute growth plan.
   - Cons: Dilution; fundraising timeline & market conditions matter.

12. Venture debt
   - Pros: Non-dilutive equity (mostly), typically interest-only early; can extend runway 6–18 months depending on size.
   - Cons: Covenants; requires predictable revenue; fees and interest.

13. Strategic MRR/enterprise deals, prepayments, or annual contracts with discounts
   - Impact: Close 1–2 large prepay deals can eliminate the need to cut aggressively.
   - Pros: Revenue-backed; strengthens customer relationships.
   - Cons: Sales cycle lead time; may require short-term discounting.

14. Monetization moves: price increases, packaging changes, upsells
   - Impact: Small price increase (e.g., 5–10%) can materially increase gross margin if churn is low.
   - Pros: Improves revenue without fundraising.
   - Cons: Risk of churn; needs careful communication.

One-time or creative options
- R&D / payroll tax credits, refundable tax credits, grants — one-time cash inflows.
- Sale & leaseback of equipment, or sale of non-core assets.
- Customer financing / factoring of receivables (if DSO is large).

Sample blended recommendation (practical and balanced)
Objective: extend runway to ~30 months while preserving core team and growth.

- Immediate (0–30d): hiring freeze, cut 40% of discretionary spend (marketing/travel/events), cancel unused SaaS. Estimated burn reduction 10–15%.
- Short-term (30–90d): renegotiate vendor contracts and payment terms, accelerate collections/DSO improvement initiatives. Estimated additional 5%.
- Medium-term (60–120d): seek a $700K bridge/venture debt which covers ~11 months buffer; simultaneously implement a targeted 10–15% headcount optimization only if revenue targets are not met. Combined financing + 20–30% cumulative cost saves to reach ~30 months runway.
- Sales: run a focused enterprise/renewal push to land prepay/annual deals (incentive plan to accelerate closings).

