Good summary — this gives a strong starting point. Below I’ll (1) reconcile the headline numbers, (2) run quick scenarios showing how different cost cuts / raises extend runway, (3) list prioritized cost-reduction and runway-extension levers (with practical actions), and (4) give a concise finance narrative + suggested messages/slides you can use in internal materials.

Quick reconciliation (so we share the same baseline)
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Stated burn: $62,000 / month
  - Note: 1.2M / 62K = 19.35 months (≈19.4 months). You quoted 20 months — that’s fine (rounding or small receivable/timing differences). Confirm whether your stated burn is net of revenue (i.e., monthly net cash outflow) or gross cash outflow. I’ll assume burn = net cash burn (cash leaving company after receipts).

Runway sensitivity (using $1.2M cash)
- Current runway = 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months
Scenarios (impact of permanent changes to monthly burn)
- -10% burn → new burn = $55,800 ⇒ runway ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- -20% burn → new burn = $49,600 ⇒ runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- -30% burn → new burn = $43,400 ⇒ runway ≈ 27.7 months (+8.3 months)

Fundraising gap examples (if you prefer to raise cash instead of / in addition to cuts)
- To secure 24 months at current burn: need 62K * 24 = $1,488,000 → raise ≈ $288K
- To secure 36 months at current burn: need 62K * 36 = $2,232,000 → raise ≈ $1,032K

Combined approach example
- A 20% cut (new burn $49.6K) + raise $600K ⇒ new runway = (1.2M + 600K) / 49.6K ≈ 36.3 months.
This shows modest raises plus disciplined cuts multiply runway.

Prioritized levers to extend runway (ranked, with actions and expected magnitude)
1) Immediate cash-conservation moves (high impact, fast)
   - Hiring freeze for all non-critical roles (effect: saves immediate monthly pay run; typically 5–15% of burn depending on headcount mix).
   - Pause discretionary spend: conferences, marketing pilots, travel, recruiting agency fees (low-friction cuts).
   - SaaS & vendor audit: cancel or downgrade underused subscriptions; renegotiate top 5 vendor contracts for price/terms (savings 3–8%).
   - Suspend non-essential capital and strategic spend (defers cash outflow).
   - Extend vendor payment terms where possible (convert cash outflow timing).
2) Revenue / cash acceleration (high ROI, moderate effort)
   - Accelerate collections: tighten AR, offer 1–2% early-pay discounts, require partial upfront on new deals (reduces working capital).
   - Focus a 30-day “close biggest deals” sales sprint: prioritize enterprise contracts with upfront payments or annual prepayment discounts.
   - Launch targeted pricing / packaging changes or upsell campaigns to top customers (short-term lift).
3) Controllable operating cuts (medium impact, moderate difficulty)
   - Reduce contractor/consultant spend and convert to lower-cost alternatives.
   - Consolidate roles / restructure teams (optimize headcount mix; may save 10–25% of payroll if deep cuts are needed).
   - Implement temporary salary reductions or voluntary unpaid leave programs (good for short-term runway relief; needs strong communication).
4) Financing / external liquidity (fast to medium; trade-offs include dilution/cost)
   - Small bridge round from existing investors (fast, lower dilution than open round).
   - Convertible notes or SAFEs (quick execution).
   - Venture debt (non-dilutive, requires solid revenue/metrics; may have covenants).
   - Revenue-based financing or factoring AR (fast cash, higher cost).
   - Strategic partnership with upfront payment or distribution agreement.
5) Structural / longer-term options (time-consuming)
   - Product rationalization: sunset low-margin or low-adoption products.
   - Strategic M&A (sell non-core assets or seek strategic buyer for business unit).
   - Seek grants or non-dilutive financing where available.

Practical estimated impact (example numbers)
- Hiring freeze + pause discretionary spend + SaaS/vendor cleanup = 10–15% burn reduction ≈ $6.2K–9.3K / month → extends runway ~1–3 months.
- Deeper headcount+reorg (15–25% savings) → adds 3–7 months runway.
- Small bridge raise $300K ≈ adds ~4.8 months at current burn (300K / 62K ≈ 4.8 months) or reduces the raise required if combined with modest cuts.

Finance narrative — structure + sample language
Structure (one-slide or one-page)
1. Current position (clear, factual)
2. Key risk / objective (what we must achieve)
3. Options considered (cuts vs growth vs financing)
4. Recommendation (what we will do, timeline)
5. Impact & metrics (runway after plan, milestones)
6. Ask (board approval, authority to hire/fire, permission to raise)

Sample concise narrative (for board/leadership slide)
- Current: “As of today Stellar Labs holds $1.2M in cash with a current net cash burn of $62K/month (≈19.4 months runway). Revenue last quarter was $670K.”
- Objective: “Maintain 24–36 months of runway while preserving growth momentum in priority product lines.”
- Recommendation (combined): “Implement an immediate cost-conservation package (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiation, pause discretionary spend) targeting a ~15–20% burn reduction, and concurrently pursue a $500–700K bridge from existing investors or a short convertible note. This combination targets ~30–36 months of runway.”
- Key milestones / KPIs: “Monthly burn, cash on hand, revenue growth rate, top-10 AR days, number of closed enterprise prepayments. Report weekly in finance check-ins; board update in 30 days.”
- Risks & mitigations: “Sales slowdown → trigger contingency cuts; inability to raise → prepare deeper headcount realignment.”

Messaging templates (short)
- Board update opening: “We have $1.2M cash and a net burn of $62K/month. To increase resilience and reach our next growth milestone, we recommend a combined plan of immediate cost discipline plus a targeted bridge raise. Details and scenarios follow.”
- All-hands (employee) reassurance: “Our business remains healthy; quarterly revenue was $670K. We are initiating temporary cost-savings and efficiency measures to strengthen our runway and invest in strategic priorities. Leadership will share details and answer questions in a follow-up Q&A.”
- Investor outreach subject / opener: “Quick update: Stellar Labs current cash $1.2M; planning a small bridge to extend runway while we execute growth initiatives. Would you be open to a short call to discuss a ~$500K convertible bridge?”

Suggested slide/table content to include in internal materials
- One-page snapshot: Cash, burn, runway, revenue last quarter, net new MRR (if SaaS), top cash line items.
- Runway sensitivity table (current, -10%, -20%, -30% burn; +$250K raise; combined scenarios).
- Action plan timeline (0–30 / 30–60 / 60–90 days) with owners.
- KPIs: Monthly burn, cash balance, revenue by product, gross margin, AR days, largest contracts in pipeline + probability & expected cash timing.
- Decision triggers: e.g., “If bridge raise not secured by Day 30, implement contingency Plan B (20% deeper cuts)”.

Next steps I recommend (concrete)
1. Confirm the definition of burn (is the $62K net of receipts? any seasonal variance?) and whether any cash is restricted/committed.
2. Provide a 1-2 page P&L and cash-flow (last 6 months + forecast next 6 months) so we can model precise ramp / seasonality.
3. Decide target runway you want (24 or 36 months are common board targets).
4. Choose approach preference: purely cuts, purely raise, or hybrid — I’ll build the model and a short slide deck / board memo and suggested investor pitch language.
5. If you want, I can draft:
   - A 1-page board memo recommending a specific plan with the scenarios,
   - An all-hands script to communicate to employees,
   - A templated investor email for a bridge raise.

Questions for you (so I tailor the materials)
- Is the $62K burn net of revenue (i.e., cash burn after receipts)? If not, please share the net cash burn number.
- Any upcoming one-time cash needs (large vendor payments, capex, legal)?
- Are existing investors likely to participate in a bridge?
- What runway target does leadership prefer (24 months? 36 months?) and which levers are off-limits (e.g., layoffs)?

If you say “yes” to any of the drafts above, tell me which (board memo, employee script, investor email) and confirm the burn-definition and runway target — I’ll prepare the materials next.

