Helpful summary first, then concrete scenarios, prioritized levers (with estimated impact ranges and trade-offs), a short finance narrative you can drop into materials, KPIs to track, and a sample email/ask to leadership. I’m assuming “burn rate $62K/month” is net burn (expenses minus revenue). If that’s not right, tell me and I’ll redo the numbers.

Quick facts / baseline
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Net burn: $62,000/month
- Runway = cash / net burn = 1,200,000 / 62,000 ≈ 19.4 months (you reported 20 months — consistent with rounding)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → ~223,333/month
- Implied monthly expenses = revenue + net burn ≈ 223,333 + 62,000 = 285,333

Objective suggestions
- Short-term objective (immediate): protect cash and add 3–6 months of runway with low-friction actions (hiring freeze, vendor renegotiation, pause discretionary spend).
- Medium-term objective (30–36 months runway): combine expense cuts + accelerate revenue growth or partial fundraising.
- Target runway to present: recommend asking for a board/leadership goal (common targets: 24 months minimum; 30–36 months comfortable). I’ll prepare scenarios to show how to reach those targets.

Scenario math (clear, repeatable)
A. Baseline
- Runway ≈ 19.4 months.

B. If you reduce net burn directly
- Cut net burn 10% → new burn = 55,800 → runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- Cut net burn 25% → new burn = 46,500 → runway ≈ 25.8 months (+6.4 months)
- Cut net burn 50% → new burn = 31,000 → runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.3 months)

C. If you cut total operating expenses (E = ~285,333/month)
- 10% cut in total expenses → E' = 256,800 → new net burn = 256,800 − 223,333 = 33,467 → runway ≈ 35.9 months
- Break-even (zero net burn) occurs when E' = revenue (223,333). That requires a reduction of ~21.7% of total expenses (62,000/month). So ~21.7% expense cut gets you to cash-neutral operations.
Note: cutting total expenses has a bigger leverage effect here because revenue is already high relative to current net burn.

D. Revenue growth scenarios (hold expenses constant)
- 10% increase in monthly revenue → +22,333/month → new revenue = 245,666 → new burn = 285,333 − 245,666 = 39,667 → runway ≈ 30.3 months
- 20% increase → new burn ≈ 17,333 → runway ≈ 69.2 months

E. Combined example (moderate expense cut + revenue growth)
- 10% expense cut + 10% revenue growth → expenses = 256,800; revenue = 245,666 → new burn ≈ 11,134 → runway ≈ 107.8 months (be careful: combined actions can rapidly turn into a large positive cash margin).

Action levers (prioritized by speed + expected impact). I’ve given typical ranges; adapt to your P&L.
Immediate / Quick wins (0–30 days)
- Hiring freeze on open roles (saves full hire cost; typical savings $10k–$50k+/mo depending on roles).
- Pause non-essential contractor spend and marketing campaigns (saves $10k–$60k/mo).
- Vendor & SaaS renegotiation / cancel unused licenses (saves $5k–$25k/mo).
- Capex and travel freeze (one-time and monthly savings $5k–$20k).
Impact: low risk, fast to implement. Should be presented as temporary unless deeper cuts are approved.

Near term (30–90 days)
- Headcount right-sizing where roles are duplicated or low ROI (biggest single lever; could save 30–60% of personnel cost if aggressively applied).
- Salary deferrals, reduced bonuses, or temporary furloughs (less permanent than layoffs; moderate morale risk).
- Renegotiate office lease or sublet space (saves depending on lease).
Impact: higher savings but higher people / morale impact. Requires clear governance and communications.

Strategic / Medium term (90–180 days)
- Reprioritize product roadmap — pause lower ROI projects (reallocate development spend).
- Rework commercial strategy to accelerate higher-margin revenue (pricing changes, upsells).
- Consider M&A or asset sales of non-core IP/units.
Impact: sustainable savings or revenue upside but take time.

Capital & financing levers
- Bridge or equity raise (term sheets, up to desired cash + runway). Present as parallel track if market conditions allow.
- Venture debt or convertible notes (less dilution but repayment covenant risk).
- Strategic partnerships or non-dilutive grants.
Impact: fastest to add cash but requires market access and may dilute. Use to buy time while operational fixes are implemented.

Trade-offs and governance
- Any growth cuts (marketing/BDR) slow revenue ramp — quantify ROI by channel and protect high-ROI initiatives.
- Personnel cuts reduce capacity; tie to a 3-6 month project prioritization plan.
- Communication plan: be transparent with managers and board; craft messaging internally to minimize attrition.

Finance narrative (short paragraph you can drop into internal materials)
Stellar Labs enters this planning period with $1.2M in cash and a current net burn of $62k/month, giving approximately 19–20 months of runway. Revenue last quarter was strong at $670k (≈ $223k/month), and a targeted set of operational actions can materially extend runway or reach cash-neutral operations. Specifically, a ~22% reduction in total operating expenses would reach monthly breakeven; smaller, targeted expense reductions combined with modest revenue growth (e.g., 10% rev growth + 10% expense savings) push us to a sustainable positive cash position. We recommend an immediate hiring freeze and vendor review, a 30–60 day expense audit per cost center, and a contingency plan that sequences deeper cuts only if fundraising or revenue acceleration stalls. Requested leadership decisions: approve immediate hiring freeze, authorize vendor renegotiation, and set a runway target (recommended minimum 24 months, preferred 30–36 months) to guide next actions.

Suggested KPIs & charts for the deck
- Cash balance (month by month, actual vs. scenario)
- Net burn (monthly)
- Revenue (monthly) and revenue-growth scenarios
- Month-by-month runway under baseline and two planned scenarios (conservative & aggressive)
- Headcount and FTE cost by department
- Top 5 vendors / major recurring costs and potential savings
- AR aging, deferred revenue, and major receivable concentrations

Sample concise ask to leadership / board (email snippet)
Subject: Recommended near-term financial plan & runway extension options
Body (short):
We have $1.2M in cash and are burning ~$62k/month (≈19–20 months runway). Revenue last quarter was $670k. I recommend we (1) implement an immediate hiring freeze, (2) start a 30-day vendor & subscription audit, and (3) run a cost-per-center review with the goal of identifying ≥15% OpEx savings within 60 days. These steps — combined with focused commercial initiatives — can get us to a 24–36 month runway without jeopardizing our highest-priority growth initiatives. I’ll circulate a slide deck with scenario modeling and a proposed decision timeline; please confirm if you approve the hiring freeze and vendor renegotiation starting today.

What I can do next (pick one)
- Build a one-page scenario slide with the numbers above and 3 board-ready scenarios (conservative, base, aggressive).
- Build a more detailed P&L-driven sensitivity model — I’ll need cost-center detail (personnel by department, vendor spend, marketing spend, planned hires, debt obligations).
- Draft a board memo + appendix with actions, timelines, and RACI.

Which would you like me to prepare next? If you want the detailed model, please upload the latest P&L / cost-center breakdown (or tell me the rough % split of expenses: people / marketing / product / G&A).

