Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help. Below I’ll (A) confirm the math and key assumptions, (B) show runway-extension scenarios with specific reduction targets and required monthly savings, (C) list prioritized levers (quick wins → structural changes) with estimated impact ranges, and (D) give a tight, copy-ready finance narrative / slide notes you can use in internal materials. If you want, I can turn this into a slide deck or a one-page memo next.

A. Numbers & assumptions
- Cash: $1.2M
- Stated burn: $62K/month (I’ll treat this as net cash burn = cash outflows minus operating inflows)
- Current runway (at $62K/month) = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months (rounded = 19.4 months). Your “20 months” is consistent with rounding.
- Revenue last quarter = $670K → monthly revenue ≈ $223K (670K / 3). Note: Revenue level matters only insofar as it affects net burn; please confirm whether the reported burn already accounts for that revenue.

If the burn figure is gross instead of net, or if significant seasonality or near-term contract expirations exist, let me know — that changes the plan and urgency.

B. Scenario analysis — monthly burn vs runway
Goal: show impact of reducing burn by X% (rounded):

- Current: $62,000 → runway ≈ 19.4 months
- −10% burn ($55,800) → runway ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- −20% burn ($49,600) → runway ≈ 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
- −30% burn ($43,400) → runway ≈ 27.7 months (+8.3 months)
- −35% burn ($40,300) → runway ≈ 29.8 months (≈ 30 months)
- −40% burn ($37,200) → runway ≈ 32.3 months

Target examples:
- To get to 24 months runway → need monthly burn ≈ 1,200,000 / 24 = $50,000 → ~$12K/month reduction (~19.4% cut).
- To get to 30 months runway → need monthly burn ≈ $40,000 → ~ $22K/month reduction (~35.5% cut).

C. Prioritized levers to extend runway (organized by speed / cost to implement)
