2) Quick scenario math (how much runway you get)
- Baseline: 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.4 months.
- If you reduce net monthly burn by:
  - 10% (new burn = $55,800) → runway = 1,200,000 / 55,800 = 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
  - 20% (new burn = $49,600) → runway = 24.2 months (+4.8 months)
  - 25% (new burn = $46,500) → runway = 25.8 months (+6.4 months)
  - 33% (new burn = $41,540) → runway = 28.9 months (+9.5 months)
  - 50% (new burn = $31,000) → runway = 38.7 months (+19.3 months)
- One-time cash savings: every $100k of one-time savings or deferred spend = +1.61 months of runway (100k / 62k).
- Fundraising to reach runway targets (no cost change):
  - To hit 24 months: need cash = 24 * 62k = $1,488,000 → additional ≈ $288k
  - To hit 30 months: need cash = 30 * 62k = $1,860,000 → additional ≈ $660k
  - To hit 36 months: need cash = 36 * 62k = $2,232,000 → additional ≈ $1,032k
- Revenue increases: because burn is net, each $1 of sustained additional net revenue reduces net burn by $1/month, extending runway proportionally. Example: add $25k/month net revenue → new net burn = 37k → runway = 1.2M / 37k ≈ 32.4 months.

3) Practical runway-extension levers (prioritized, with typical impact & timing)
A. Immediate / Quick wins (realize in days–6 weeks)
- Pause discretionary spend (travel, noncritical marketing campaigns): savings $5k–$40k/month depending on current spend.
- Hiring freeze and pause contractor approvals: save $10k–$100k/month depending on open roles vs contractors. Very fast.
- Delay non-essential capital spend and vendor payments where possible (negotiate terms): one-time cash improvement $50k–$300k.
- Renegotiate SaaS & vendor contracts (consolidate licenses, annual vs monthly): 10–30% on vendor spend, effective 30–60 days.
- Collections/AR acceleration: tighten payment terms, incentives for early pay — improves cash conversion within 30 days.

B. Near-term / Medium impact (4–12 weeks)
- Reduce marketing spend to high-ROI channels only; reallocate to sales enablement & pipeline acceleration. Savings vary; aim 15–30% of Marketing Opex.
- Trim non-essential headcount or role consolidation (if necessary): meaningful recurring savings; depends on org structure. One-off severance costs but long-term burn reduction.
- Reprice or upsell to existing customers (short sales cycles): immediate revenue uplift if product-market fit supports it.
- Convert fixed costs to variable (outsourcing, usage-based cloud pricing): lower fixed burn, improves flexibility.

C. Strategic / Longer-term (2–6+ months)
- Fundraise (convertible note, bridge, or priced round) — raises runway quickly but dilutive/costly. Target amount = desired runway gap + buffer.
- Pursue non-dilutive capital (grants, tax credits, customer pre-payments/annual billing): favorable but longer to set up.
- Strategic partnerships or channel deals to accelerate bookings (may take 3–6 months).
- Product rationalization (sunset low-ROI products) and process automation to permanently reduce cost of goods sold.

