Great — this is a good starting position. Below I lay out (1) quick sanity-checked math, (2) quantified scenario analysis for runway under realistic cost / revenue moves, (3) concrete cost-reduction and runway-extension levers you can use, (4) recommended finance narrative for internal stakeholders (executive / board / company), and (5) next steps and requests for any missing detail so I can prepare slides/deliverables.

1) Quick numbers check
- Cash: $1,200,000
- Reported burn: $62,000 / month
  - Runway = cash / burn = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = ~19.4 months (your “20 months” is a rounded figure — consistent).
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → ~ $223,333 / month (quarterly revenue / 3). Note: I’m treating burn as net cash burn; if that burn is gross (before revenue), the runway calculation changes. Confirm whether burn is net of revenue.

2) Runway sensitivity scenarios (simple, net-burn approach)
- Baseline: burn $62k → runway ≈ 19.4 months
- 10% cost reduction → burn $55.8k → runway ≈ 21.5 months (+2.1 months)
- 25% cost reduction → burn $46.5k → runway ≈ 25.8 months (+6.4 months)
- 50% cost reduction → burn $31k → runway ≈ 38.7 months (+19.3 months)

If you can convert customers to annual prepayments (or accelerate collections) equal to an additional $200k/month in cash inflow (example):
- Net burn reduces from $62k to negative (cash positive), but realistic targets are smaller. Example: $50k/month extra → net burn = $12k/month → runway = 1.2M/12k = 100 months (but more realistically you’d reinvest some of that incremental revenue).

3) Concrete levers to extend runway (prioritized, with estimated impact ranges)
A. Immediate / low-friction (0–3 months)
- Hiring freeze (stop all non-critical hires) — ~0–10% immediate reduction in monthly payroll hiring spend.
- Pause discretionary spend: travel, conferences, contractor ramp-ups — ~1–5% cost reduction.
- SaaS/vendor audit & renegotiate: eliminate redundant services, negotiate lower rates or annual prepay discounts — ~1–5% savings.
- Collections push: accelerate A/R, shorten terms, offer 1–2% discount for earlier payment — improves cash conversion, one-time bump to cash.
Impact: 5–10% monthly burn reduction typically.

B. Near-term / moderate friction (1–3 months to implement)
- Reduce contractor / consultant spend, move to fixed-scope engagements — ~3–8%.
- Cloud and infra optimization (rightsizing, reserved instances, shut down non-prod) — ~2–6%.
- Marketing reallocation: pause underperforming channels, focus on high ROI account work — ~1–5%.
Impact: additional 5–15%.

C. Structural / higher friction (3–8 weeks decision + execution)
- Compensation adjustments: temporary salary reductions, defer bonuses, alter equity mix — ~5–15% depending on approach and scope.
- Restructuring / headcount reductions for redundant roles or low-priority projects — large single-step savings; typically 10–30% of payroll if applied aggressively.
- Office footprint reduction / renegotiate leases (if applicable) — meaningful ongoing savings if you can move remote or sublease.
Impact: 10–30+% depending on depth.

D. Revenue & financing levers
- Accelerate sales: incentives for annual or multi-year prepayments, upsell programs, fast-track enterprise deals with concessions for cash up-front.
- Short-term deals: limited-time discounts for annual prepayment, or professional services pre-buys.
- Financing: venture debt (preserve equity; need revenue traction/covenants), SAFE / convertible bridge round, revenue-based financing, strategic partner prepay or customer-backed loans.
- Non-dilutive: grants, government R&D credits, tax refunds, factoring A/R (careful with cost).

4) Recommended prioritized plan (what to do, in which order)
Phase 1 — Immediate (week 0–2)
- Confirm burn is net of revenue; prepare a 1-page cash flow forecast for 12 months (actuals + expected inflows). I can draft this.
- Announce hiring freeze and pause discretionary spend.
- Run vendor/SaaS audit and quick cloud cost sweep.
- Collections drive: prioritize outstanding, push for prepayments where possible.

Phase 2 — 30–60 days
- Implement negotiated vendor savings and cloud commitments.
- Re-assess product roadmap; defer non-core projects; reassign headcount to revenue-generating / retention efforts.
- Offer customers incentives for annual prepayment or expanded scope for cash.
- Build a detailed 12–24 month scenario model (best / base / downside) and target fundraising need.

Phase 3 — 60–120 days
- If runway still needs extension, prepare fundraising deck and target 18–24 months post-money runway. Consider venture debt as bridge to extend time with limited dilution.
- If necessary, implement more structural cost actions (comp adjustments, role reductions) with prepared employee communication.

5) Finance narrative / talking points
Executive summary (2–3 sentences)
- “Stellar Labs is in a strong position with $1.2M cash and roughly 20 months of runway at our current net burn of $62k/month. We will take immediate, low-friction measures to preserve cash while simultaneously accelerating revenue collection. We are targeting a 10–25% reduction in net burn within the next 60 days to build optionality and reduce the need for near-term dilution.”

Why we’re taking action
- “20 months is comfortable, but market conditions and execution risk argue for prudence. Small cost actions now significantly extend runway and create leverage for better fundraising terms or allow us to execute growth initiatives from a position of strength.”

What we will do (bullets)
- Immediate: hiring freeze; pause non-essential spend; collections acceleration; vendor renegotiations.
- Near-term: cloud/infra optimization; refocus R&D to revenue-generating features; incentivize annual prepayments.
- Contingent: prepare fundraising plan sized to provide 18–24 months runway post-close; consider venture debt or customer prepayments before headcount reductions.

Impact (quantified)
- “A 10% reduction in burn extends runway to ~21.5 months; 25% reduction extends runway to ~25.8 months. Combined with incremental annual prepayments from customers, we can achieve a 24+ month runway without structural layoffs.”

Employee messaging (short, empathetic)
- “We’re in a strong cash position but will take prudent steps to strengthen our runway and protect the company. We’re pausing hiring, reducing discretionary spending, and asking teams to prioritize customer-facing and revenue-driving work. No immediate changes to payroll are planned; any necessary measures will be communicated transparently.”

6) Suggested slide deck / one-pager structure for internal financial materials
- Slide 1: Executive summary & ask (what you want from execs/board)
- Slide 2: Current position (cash, burn, runway, revenue trend graph)
- Slide 3: Key assumptions (define net/before revenue, seasonality)
- Slide 4: Scenario analysis (baseline, -10%, -25%, -50% cost cuts and revenue accelerations)
- Slide 5: Top levers & estimated timeline for each (categorized immediate/near-term/structural)
- Slide 6: Proposed action plan (phases, owners, deadlines)
- Slide 7: Fundraising / financing plan (target amounts, timing, options)
- Slide 8: Risks & mitigations
- Appendix: detailed monthly cash flow model, cost-breakdown, sensitivity tables

7) Risks & trade-offs
- Aggressive cost cuts reduce runway risk but may slow growth/revenue and lower buyer interest in fundraising.
- Raising debt increases fixed obligations; equity rounds dilute ownership.
- Incentivizing annual prepayments often requires pricing concessions; calculate NPV vs runway benefit.

8) What I need from you to produce slide-ready materials / a cash model
- Confirm: Is the $62K burn net of revenue (monthly cash outflow after revenue), or is it gross burn (payroll + opex before revenue)?
- Monthly revenue breakdown and trend (past 6–12 months), plus churn / top customers if available.
- Payroll & headcount by function and monthly payroll cost.
- Recurring operating expenses (SaaS, cloud, leases) summary.
- Any committed funding or near-term large customer payments.
- Audience (board, execs, all-company?) and deadline.

If you want, I can:
- Draft the 8-slide deck and a 12-month monthly cashflow model using the baseline assumptions above.
- Prepare a one-page executive memo + suggested employee announcement language.

Next step: tell me which deliverable you want first (slide deck, one-pager, detailed model) and confirm whether burn is net of revenue and provide any of the items in section 8. I’ll build the model and a recommended narrative.

