  - Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → average monthly revenue ≈ $223,333/month (670k / 3). (If this changes the net burn calculation, please clarify whether the $62K is after revenue.)

Runway-extension scenarios (if net burn = $62K/month)
- No change: 19.4 months
- Cost reduction only:
  - 10% burn cut → burn = $55.8K → runway = 21.5 months
  - 20% cut → burn = $49.6K → runway = 24.2 months
  - 30% cut → burn = $43.4K → runway = 27.7 months
  - 50% cut → burn = $31.0K → runway = 38.7 months
- Revenue uplift only (extra net revenue applied to reduce burn):
  - +$25K/month → burn = $37K → runway = 32.4 months
  - +$50K/month → burn = $12K → runway = 100 months
  - +$62K/month → burn = $0 → break-even (cash preserved; runway not decreasing)
- Example combined (realistic): 20% cost cut (to $49.6K) + +$25K/month incremental revenue → net burn = $24.6K → runway ≈ 48.8 months

Notes:
- If the $62K burn is pre-revenue (i.e., gross opex), we need to subtract revenue to compute net burn. Tell me how burn is defined in your internal reporting and I’ll recalc precisely.
- These are deterministic, single-line scenarios—real life will include timing of revenues, one-time severance, tax impacts, and financing costs.

B — Practical levers to extend runway (ranked by speed / impact)
Immediate (0–30 days) — fast cash preservation (low implementation friction)
- Hiring freeze + pause all non-critical offers (immediate, high impact if hiring planned).
- Pause or reduce contractor/agency spend; renegotiate scope/rates (quick savings).
- Cut discretionary spend: travel, events, conferences, expensive marketing pilots.
- Freeze new capital projects and non-essential software purchases.
- Accelerate collections: prioritize outstanding AR, offer short-term payment incentives for prepayment.
- Push out payables where possible (negotiate extended terms with vendors).
- Sponsor short-term contingency: temporary, voluntary salary deferrals or reduced hours (clear plan and sunset).

Near-term (30–90 days) — structural savings and revenue moves
- Vendor/SaaS rationalization: identify top 10 subscriptions and cut/renegotiate (likely 5–15% savings).
- Real estate / office optimization: partial remote policy or renegotiate lease (if applicable).
- Marketing reallocation: pause low-return channels; double down on highest-ROI campaigns.
- Sales acceleration: shorten sales cycle, focus on upsells, create offers (annual prepay discounts, bundling) — target measurable $ added MRR.
- Product monetization: identify features that can be monetized quickly or higher tier pricing.
- Revisit benefits and perks (non-headcount costs) for short-term savings.
- Efficiency program: 1–2 week review of top expense categories with owners and measurable targets.

Structural / Medium-term (90+ days)
- Organizational restructuring if necessary (consolidate roles, remove duplicative functions) — use attrition first where possible to reduce severance.
- Outsourcing/nearshoring for non-core functions.
- Longer-term strategic partnerships or channel distribution to accelerate recurring revenue.
- Repricing and packaging strategy to lift ARPU / churn improvements.

C — Financing options (tradeoffs)
- Bridge round (SAFE/convertible note): quick, less negotiation, dilutive later — good to buy time for growth.
- Equity raise (Seed/Series A extension): longer timeline, more dilution, resets runway substantially.
- Venture debt: non-dilutive short-term capital, requires covenants and typically revenue/recurring revenue history.
- Revenue-based financing: repay from future revenues, non-dilutive but more expensive than bank debt.
- Customer prepayments / annual contracts at discount: helps cash now and improves revenue visibility.
- Strategic partnership or corporate investor: can include commercial commitments and immediate cash; may come with strategic constraints.
- Grants/non-dilutive public funding: slower and small, but useful to complement.

