If anything below should use different assumptions (for example if the $62K is gross burn vs net burn, or revenue is seasonal/non-recurring), tell me and I’ll re-run.

A — Key numbers & sanity check
- Cash on hand: $1,200,000
- Reported runway: 20 months (you stated this)
- Reported burn: $62,000 / month (I’ll assume this is net burn = cash out minus cash in)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → average revenue ≈ $670,000 / 3 = $223,333 / month

Check: runway = cash / burn = 1,200,000 / 62,000 = 19.35 months → rounds to ~19–20 months (your 20 months is consistent).

B — Runway-extension targets (how much net-burn reduction you need)
Goal: reduce net burn (B) so runway = cash / B = target months.

- To reach 24 months: required burn = 1,200,000 / 24 = $50,000 → must reduce net burn by $12,000 / month (≈ 19.4% reduction).
- To reach 30 months: required burn = 1,200,000 / 30 = $40,000 → reduce net burn by $22,000 / month (≈ 35.5%).
- To reach 36 months: required burn = 1,200,000 / 36 = $33,333 → reduce net burn by $28,667 / month (≈ 46.2%).

If instead you raise additional capital, every $1M raised extends runway by ≈ 1,000,000 / 62,000 ≈ 16.1 months (assuming the burn rate stays the same).

C — Concrete levers to achieve those reductions (with typical / illustrative savings)
I’ll group actions into quick wins (low friction), medium effort, and structural changes (have bigger impact but more disruptive).

Quick wins (implement in 0–30 days)
- Hiring freeze and pause open reqs — immediate monthly saving: ~$10k per paused headcount (example: fully loaded $120k/yr ≈ $10k/mo). If you have 2 open roles, pause saves ≈ $20k/mo. 
- Freeze contractor / consultant spend — typical savings: $5–30k/mo depending on contractor reliance.
- Stop discretionary spend (events, travel, non-critical marketing experiments) — $5–25k/mo.
- Software subscription audit — cancel underused tools and consolidate: $1–8k/mo.
- Accelerate AR & collections; offer small discount for annual prepay — can create immediate cash inflow and reduce short-term burn.

Medium-term (30–90 days)
- Vendor & SaaS renegotiations / consolidated procurement — 5–20% saving on relevant line items.
- Cloud & infra cost optimization (rightsizing, reserved instances, caching, storage tiering) — often 10–40% of cloud bill (savings example $2–20k/mo depending on scale).
- Marketing reallocation toward highest ROI channels, pause low-ROI campaigns — reduce spend while protecting revenue pipeline.
- Sales incentives for prepaid / multi-year deals (give 3–8% discount for 12-month prepay) — immediate cash vs small margin impact.
- Reduce office footprint / shift to partial remote (if leases allow) — can defer or reduce rent costs.

Structural / higher-impact (90+ days)
- Organizational restructuring / headcount reduction (if necessary) — one headcount = ~$10k/mo saved (see above); larger reductions are proportionally greater. Factor severance and morale impact.
- Outsource non-core functions / convert FTE to contractors in specific areas — variable savings.
- Product roadmap reprioritization: delay lower ROI development to reduce spend.

Revenue / cash-raising levers
- Secure annual prepaid contracts with largest customers (offer 3–8% discount) — immediate cash.
- Upsell existing customers / packaging add-ons — faster conversion and higher ARPU.
- Short-term price adjustments for new customers or new seats (communicate carefully).
- Strategic partnerships (co-funded projects, reseller deals) and channel expansion.
- Financing: bridge/seed round, convertible note / SAFE, venture debt, revenue-based financing, grants — tradeoffs: dilution, covenants, cost of capital.
  - Example: $1M convertible note → ~16 months extra runway at flat burn.
  - Venture debt can be non-dilutive but requires covenants and typically interest.

D — Example scenario mixes (how to get to 24 months)
Target: 24 months (reduce burn to $50k/mo from $62k):

Option A — Cost-first (fast, low friction)
- Hiring freeze + cancel contractors = save $12k/mo → achieves target.

Option B — Revenue boost + some cost cuts
- Convert one annual contract prepay with a large customer → one-time cash injection or reduce monthly net burn by $6k (effective).
- Trim marketing & discretionary spend by $6k/mo → combined $12k → achieves target.

Option C — Partial financing + smaller cuts
- Raise $400k bridge → adds ~6.45 months by itself; or
- Raise $300k + reduce burn by $6k/month → provides cushion and optionality.

E — 30/60/90-day action plan (owner, KPI)
0–30 days (Immediate)
- Implement hiring freeze (Owner: HR/CEO). KPI: # open reqs paused; monthly saving est.
- Freeze discretionary spends and audit subscriptions (Owner: Finance/Operations). KPI: $ canceled / month.
- AR push and offer annual prepay options to top 10 customers (Owner: Sales/Finance). KPI: $ collected; DSO change.
- Quick cloud and software cost audit (Owner: CTO). KPI: expected $ savings/month.

30–60 days
- Renegotiate vendor contracts and marketing supplier deals (Owner: Procurement/Marketing). KPI: % reduction in vendor spend.
- Implement targeted headcount/contractor reductions if gaps remain (Owner: CEO/People). KPI: $ saved/month; employee morale metric.
- Launch pricing/packaging tests and enterprise upsell motions (Owner: Sales/GM). KPI: ARR growth rate; average deal size.

60–90 days
- Decide fundraising path if runway still below desired target: raise bridge or seed (Owner: CEO/CFO). KPI: $ committed; time to close.
- Revisit product roadmap to optimize capital allocation (Owner: Product). KPI: roadmap cost vs ARR contribution.

KPIs to track weekly/monthly
- Net burn ($) and rolling 12-week cash flow
- Cash on hand and runway (months)
- MRR/ARR growth and churn (if SaaS)
- DSO / collections & one-time prepayments secured
- Hiring and contractor spend
- Marketing CAC and LTV payback

F — Finance narrative / talking points (short, board-ready)
Executive summary (2–3 sentences)
- “Stellar Labs currently holds $1.2M in cash and a reported net burn of $62K/month, giving ~19–20 months runway. We propose a combined plan of targeted expense rationalization, revenue acceleration (prepayments and upsells), and preparing a small bridge financing option to extend runway to at least 24–30 months while preserving core product and GTM momentum.”

Rationale (bullets)
- Preserve optionality: 24–30 month runway reduces funding urgency and allows execution on product-market fit and top-line growth.
- Protect core priorities: focus reductions on non-customer-facing or low-ROI activities, preserve R&D and key sales motions.
- Balanced approach: prefer revenue acceleration + operational savings before large-scale headcount actions; prepare financing if market windows close.

Recommended short-term plan (bullets)
- Immediate hiring freeze and cancel/renegotiate non-essential contractors (saves est. $X–Y/mo).
- Accelerate collections and sell annual prepay options to top accounts.
- Conduct vendor/SaaS audit and cloud cost optimization.
- If runway after these steps remains under target, open a bridge round ($300–500k) or explore revenue-based financing.

Risks & mitigations
- Risk: reduced hiring slows go-to-market → mitigate by prioritizing critical hires only and reallocating internal resources.
- Risk: customer reaction to price/packaging changes → mitigate with white-glove sales approach and clear communications.

Closing / ask
- “We ask the leadership team to approve (1) immediate hiring freeze, (2) a discretionary spend pause, and (3) authorization for Finance to negotiate vendor discounts and open discussions with two financing partners. We will present updated runway and scenario outcomes in 30 days.”

G — Suggested appendix items for the internal deck
- One-page cash & runway table with 3 scenarios (base, cost-cut plan, cost+revenue plan).
- Detailed line-item cost-saving opportunities and estimated monthly savings.
- Fundraising options matrix (amount, timing, pros/cons).
- 30/60/90 Gantt with owners and KPIs.

