- If monthly revenue increases and reduces net burn by $20k/month (i.e., burn falls to $42k) → runway = 1,200,000 / 42,000 = 28.6 months
- If burn goes to zero (rare short-term) → you’re cash neutral; any cash is runway lifetime until negative shocks.

3) Practical runway-extension levers — prioritized, with estimated impact & timing

A. Quick / Immediate (impact in 0–30 days)
- Hiring freeze + pause open reqs
  - Savings: depends on positions; typical hiring freeze can save $10–40k+/month depending on planned hires. Immediate.
  - Pros: quick savings. Cons: slows growth/ops capacity.
- Pause non-critical discretionary spend (travel, conferences, agency/marketing campaigns)
  - Savings: $5–20k/month typical.
  - Pros: immediate; low permanent impact. Cons: may reduce near-term demand generation.
- Renegotiate vendor/SaaS contracts; switch to annual pre-pay discounts
  - Savings: 10–30% on SaaS; $2–10k/month.
  - Pros: fast cash reduction. Cons: some one-time costs/commitments.
- Delay capital projects and non-urgent product initiatives
  - Savings: varies. Immediate effect on spend cadence.

B. Near-term (30–90 days)
- Convert contractors to temporary part-time or end low-priority contractors
  - Savings: $5–30k/month. Medium impact on delivery.
- Restructure marketing spend to performance-based (stop low-ROI channels)
  - Savings: $10–30k/month depending on scale. Protects high-ROI channels.
- Office/real-estate cost reductions: remote-first, sublet space, renegotiate lease
  - Savings: $3–20k/month. Takes weeks to months depending on lease.

C. Medium-term (2–6 months)
- Targeted headcount reductions or redeployments (minimize overlap)
  - Savings: large one (depends on people). Consider severance costs.
  - Pros: meaningful runway extension. Cons: morale/retention risk.
- Renegotiate vendor payment terms (extend payables, staged payments)
  - Improves short-term cash; relationship dependent.

D. Revenue / cash-increasing levers (fast to medium)
- Offer annual/quarterly prepaid contracts at discount to strategic customers
  - Example: offer 5–10% discount for annual prepay → immediate cash; could add $100k+ one-time cash if customers accept.
- Accelerate collections: shorten payment terms, invoice immediately, offer small early-pay discounts
  - Can materially improve near-term cash.
- Upsell existing customers / launch short-term promotions with high margin
  - If revenue is growing, net burn drops quickly.
- Push enterprise deals with upfront fees (professional services add-ons)

E. Financing options (structure, pros/cons, timing)
- Bridge round / convertible note / SAFE ($250k–$2M)
  - Pros: fastest; lower dilution than priced round if short-term; flexible.
  - Cons: interim solution, investor appetite required.
  - Timing: 4–8 weeks typical.
- Venture debt (if history of revenue/growth)
  - Pros: less dilution; can be quick if lender comfortable.
  - Cons: covenants, interest, needs predictable cash flows.
- Equity round (priced)
  - Pros: larger capital; long runway. Cons: takes time (2–3+ months), dilution.
- Strategic investor / corporate partnership (co-marketing, prepayments, revenue share)
  - Pros: potential revenue or larger checks, strategic benefits.
  - Cons: negotiating complexity.
- Grants / government relief if you qualify
  - Pros: non-dilutive. Cons: slow and limited.

F. One-time / non-core options
